	abstract = {Open-pit mining sites have to be monitored for environmental management, regulatory compliance, and asset tracking, yet reviewing aerial images of large pits, excavation zones, and extraction areas by hand takes a great deal of time and effort. We propose a real-time framework that runs on edge hardware and detects open-pit mines with a fine-tuned YOLO model. The model was trained on aerial images showing many kinds of mining landscapes, site layouts, and terrain. We then deployed it on an NVIDIA Jetson Nano to see whether it could serve in the field and on autonomous surveillance drones. On the validation set, the fine-tuned model achieves a mean average precision of 56.5 percent at an IoU threshold of 0.5. After INT8 quantization, it processes 34.4 frames per second on the Jetson Nano, with an inference latency of 29.1 ms. The results suggest that pairing a lightweight detector with embedded hardware acceleration is a workable approach to aerial monitoring of mines on low-power devices. © (2026), (International Journal of Advanced Computer Science and Applications). All rights reserved.},
