	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055360550?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Road surface irregularities such as potholes, cracks, and speed bumps can have a negative impact on vehicle safety, ride comfort, and infrastructure maintenance. Conventional road surface inspection methods, which rely on expensive equipment with sporadic manual surveys, make continuous large-scale road monitoring impractical. TerraSense-AI is presented in this paper as an intelligent integrated framework for the detection and geo-localization of road surface irregularities in real-time based on the use of a smartphone's accelerometer, gyroscope and GPS sensors. The framework utilizes an integrated multi-stage processing pipeline that includes Butterworth low-pass filtering, orientation compensation, sliding-window segmentation, and feature extraction using hybrid time-, frequency- and wavelet-domain techniques. An optimized Random Forest Classifier was trained on a balanced set of 12,000 labelled events to provide accurate classification of smooth roads, potholes, speed bumps and cracks, while also maintaining low computational complexity and suitability for edge deployment. The experimental results presented for the three diverse urban road segments provided a classification accuracy of 94.7%, a weighted F1-score of 0.944, a 3.1% false positive rate, an average inference latency of 11ms and a mean geo-localization error of 2.3m. These results outperformed both conventional threshold-based and machine learning baseline methods. The proposed integrated framework provides a scalable cost-effective, and crowd-deployable solution for intelligent road condition monitoring within next-generation smart transportation systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
