	abstract = {This work addresses key limitations in state-of-the-art hand-landmark detection to enable reliable, gesture-based human-robot interaction in healthcare settings. Current gesture recognition applications fail to account for the unique characteristics of clinical scenarios, such as the requirement for healthcare professionals to wear medical gloves throughout critical stages of patient care. To mitigate this domain gap, we introduce the novel medical-glove dataset MedGlove1k and demonstrate its effectiveness in adapting hand-landmark detection models to the medical domain, while meeting crucial requirements for low latency and edge-computing deployment. After fine-tuning on MedGlove1k, our YOLO-based detection model achieves a pose mAP<sub>50</sub> of 97.4% while maintaining an inference speed of ≈ 200 frames per second, indicating an improvement of 42.4% in pose estimation performance over non-domain-adapted baselines. We further validate our approach under edge-deployment conditions, observing full-architecture latencies of 330.02 ms on an NVIDIA Jetson Nano. We demonstrate the system's practical viability by successfully integrating it into a robotic platform for real-time human-robot interaction, achieving a 96.8% success rate for end-to-end gesture-based robot control. As a foundation for future applications in healthcare settings, we make our MedGlove1k dataset publicly available and provide our detection architecture as a ROS2-based landmark and gesture recognition framework. © 2026 IEEE.},
