	abstract = {Modern automated-driving platforms increasingly rely on multimodal driver monitoring to support supervision and adaptive interaction. However, real-vehicle deployment is often hindered by dependency coupling across robotics middle-ware, sensor drivers, and heterogeneous artificial-intelligence runtimes. We validated an on-device, containerized driver-state feedback architecture that combines Robot Operating System 2 (ROS 2) acquisition with ROS-free inference containers via a shared-memory (SHM) Bridge, distilling multi-camera video and wearable physiology into compact semantic messages. Vision pipelines were evaluated across heterogeneous runtimes (OpenVINO for head pose, YOLOv8 via ONNX Runtime for person/proximity, and Ultralytics YOLOv8 for driver-object interaction), together with a physiological awareness module. The loop was closed with a local large language model (LLM), served via Ollama (Llama 3.2), to generate structured on-screen and audio feedback. In a one-hour sustained benchmark, the SHM-Bridge overhead averaged 2.45 ms (median 0.21 ms), remaining small relative to inference latency (5.23 ms physiology, 39.26 ms OpenVINO, 189.40 ms ONNX, 313.74 ms Ultralytics, and 2093.05 ms LLM). These results support SHM-Bridge decoupling as a practical strategy for modular multimodal driver monitoring in real vehicles by isolating software dependencies and mitigating runtime conflicts. © 2026 IEEE.},
