	abstract = {Accurate rock segmentation on the Martian surface is essential for autonomous path planning and safe rover navigation. This paper presents WaveMars-LiteNet, a lightweight semantic segmentation network specifically designed to address the challenges of low grayscale contrast, substantial target scale variation, and stringent computational constraints inherent in onboard planetary exploration missions. The proposed architecture integrates a multi-branch backbone with three core components: a dual-channel feature extraction module (DFEM) that enhances edge and texture discrimination, a wavelet dual-channel downsampling module (WDCDM) that fuses spatial and frequency-domain information for efficient feature reduction, and a wavelet multi-texture fusion attention module (WTFAM) that strengthens fine-grained detail representation through frequency-aware attention mechanisms. To evaluate performance under realistic operational conditions, we introduce the ZRMars-Rocks dataset, comprising annotated imagery acquired by the Zhurong Mars rover with multi-scale augmentation. Comprehensive experiments on real-world datasets (ZRMars-Rocks and MarsData-V2) and simulated benchmarks (SimMars6K and SynMars-8k) demonstrate competitive performance against representative lightweight and high-complexity segmentation models. With merely 2.38M parameters and 7.91 giga floating-point operations (GFLOPs), WaveMars-LiteNet achieves 81.68% mean intersection over union (mIoU) on ZRMars-Rocks and 93.03% on MarsData-V2. Embedded deployment evaluations on the NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX platform yield real-time inference throughputs of 71.2 frames per second (FPS) with 16-bit floating point (FP16) and 86.9 FPS with 8-bit integer (INT8) precision, with accuracy degradation constrained within 1.1%. These results indicate an accuracy-efficiency trade-off and demonstrate promising embedded feasibility for real-time rock perception under the tested rover-class constraints. © Tsinghua University Press 2026.},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Article in press},
	source = {Scopus},
	note = {Cited by: 0}
}

