	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/TENSYMP68909.2026.11704062},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055246408?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Accurate vehicle classification is a key component of Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS), supporting applications such as traffic monitoring and autonomous navigation. Although EfficientNet models perform well on classification, they demand higher memory, processing power and complexity for deployment on devices with limited resources. This paper presents a reproducible end-to-end pipeline that constructs a multi-expert EfficientNet forest framework trained with group lasso regularization, applies structured channel pruning using a conservative validation preservation rule and distills the ensemble knowledge into a compact MobileNetV3-Small student via temperature scaled KL divergence. On the Paribahan-BD dataset, the distilled student achieves a 96.5% reduction in FLOPs and a 91.6% reduction in parameters while remaining within 4.64 percentage points of the teacher's test accuracy (95.99% vs. 91.35%). Experimental results show that pruning severely degrades validation performance and that the distilled student retains competitive accuracy with substantially reduced computation and inference latency. © 2026 IEEE.},
