	abstract = {Visual fire and smoke monitoring requires detectors that retain weak cues despite background ambiguity, image degradation, and limited edge computing resources. Existing lightweight detectors leave related design and evaluation gaps. Reducing computation can weaken flame and smoke evidence, while aggregate accuracy and theoretical complexity do not characterize threshold-dependent errors, domain shift, degradation sensitivity, or measured edge efficiency. To address these gaps, we propose LFS-YOLO, a YOLO11-based detector combining efficient downsampling, local-context enhancement, semantic recalibration, and shared lightweight prediction. On the D-Fire test set, LFS-YOLO improves Precision from 81.0% to 82.4% and Recall from 72.0% to 73.7% over YOLO11n. It achieves 81.7% mAP50 and 49.5% mAP50:95[jls-end-space/], while reducing parameters and GFLOPs by 30.5% and 30.9%, respectively. On an NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano Super 8 GB operating in 25 W mode, LFS-YOLO achieves a TensorRT inference latency of 6.24 ms and 160.13 FPS. Analyses across object scales, confidence thresholds, non-fire interference, external datasets, image corruptions, and qualitative visualizations further show consistent improvements over YOLO11n and characterize the safety-relevant behavior of LFS-YOLO under diverse evaluation conditions. The results support LFS-YOLO as a compact visual detector for fire and smoke monitoring under resource-constrained conditions. © 2026 Institution of Chemical Engineers. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Final},
