	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041625038?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Structural health monitoring (SHM) systems play a crucial role in assuring the safety and availability of civil infrastructure. Nevertheless, existing SHM strategies commonly rely on threshold-based vibration evaluation or cloud-dependent processing, which bounds their potential to discover them about the early-stage defect and perform real-time localization under real-world constraints. To handle these challenges, this paper proposes an edge-intelligent structural damage localization framework which combines vibration dynamics with acoustic emission-based signal properties and deep learning-driven deduction. The presented systems acquire structural vibration outputs and high-frequency acoustic signals related to micro-crack initiation and material disruptions. Time-frequency descriptions of these signals are used to develop a deep learning model, avoiding the need for human-driven feature engineering. A lightweight feature-extracting neural network is applied at the edge to permit low-latency, on-device interpretation without continuous cloud connectivity. Damage localization is obtained by correlating responses to predefined structural zones, offering location-based insights into deterioration growth and refining early damage identification and localization precisions. © 2026 IEEE.},
