	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047242092?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Filament geometry control is a critical determinant of quality in 3D concrete printing (3DCP), where extrusion arises from tightly coupled subsystems, including robotic motion, path geometry, material delivery, and flow dynamics. Conventional modeling approaches are typically monolithic or capture only partial subsystem interactions, limiting their ability to represent the full process complexity. This study introduces a System-of-Systems (SoS) modeling framework based on a Mixture-of-Experts Long Short-Term Memory (MoE-LSTM) architecture to predict filament width as an emergent outcome of interacting subsystems. Each expert corresponds to a physically meaningful subsystem, while a gating network dynamically weights their contributions, enabling interpretable and adaptive quality modeling that captures nonlinear coupling effects during printing. For a 4-8 s prediction horizon, the MoE-LSTM achieves a mean absolute error of 0.419-0.803% with an inference latency of 0.15 ms, outperforming baseline models. A knowledge-distilled variant (KD-MoE-LSTM) further enables efficient edge deployment. © 2026 IEEE.},
