	abstract = {Driver fatigue remains a leading cause of traffic accidents in Ecuador, prompting academia and research centers to develop robust, real-time monitoring solutions. Currently, traditional methods rely on intrusive sensors or vehicle metrics susceptible to external disturbances. This article presents a non-intrusive, AI-assisted recognition framework for drowsiness detection based on computer vision and deep learning. The proposed model employs the YOLOv8 architecture, trained with labeled facial images representing different states of alertness and fatigue (closed eyes, yawning, and microsleep). The system is deployed on a Jetson Xavier NX embedded platform connected to an IMX219 camera, enabling real-time inference without relying on external servers. Additionally, a PERCLOS-based metric is integrated to quantify driver fatigue, and an alert mechanism sends automatic notifications through both a web interface and Telegram. Experimental results show that the proposed model achieves an average precision (mAP@0.5) of 0.676 and a recall of 0.67, with detection accuracies of 0.92, 0.93, and 0.74 for the closed eyes, awake, and open mouth classes, respectively. The integrated implementation sustained between 25 and 30 FPS with GPU usage below 70%, ensuring stable performance. The results confirm the feasibility of developing an efficient, low-cost, and autonomous system capable of detecting fatigue states and alerting the driver in real time, thus contributing to the reduction of traffic accidents.  © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
