	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043728449?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {With the growing demand for edge intelligence, lightweight neural networks (LNNs) have emerged as an important solution for embedded artificial intelligence applications. However, their efficient deployment on embedded ARM devices remains challenging due to limited computational capability, constrained memory resources, and strict power budgets. To address these issues, this paper proposes a multi-dimensional optimization framework that jointly integrates network structure refinement, quantization-pruning cooptimization, and ARM-oriented operator acceleration. First, an improved lightweight convolution module is designed to reduce redundant computations while preserving feature representation capability. Second, a sensitivity-guided joint quantization-pruning method is developed to compress model parameters and improve inference efficiency. Third, hardware-aware optimization is performed for ARM Cortex-A processors by exploiting NEON SIMD instructions and optimizing memory access patterns. Experimental results on three representative embedded ARM platforms, namely Raspberry Pi 5, NVIDIA Jetson Nano, and Rockchip RK3588, show that the proposed framework achieves 72.3%-81.5% model size reduction, 58.7%-69.2% inference latency reduction, and 45.1%-56.8% power reduction across the evaluated platforms, while incurring no more than 2.1 % accuracy degradation. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed framework for efficient LNN deployment on resourceconstrained embedded ARM platforms.  © 2026 IEEE.},
