	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037608092?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Visual simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) is a core capability for autonomous robots, but deploying modern deep learning based methods on resource constrained single-board computers remains challenging due to their high computational and memory demands. Recent transformer architectures improve visual odometry accuracy through global self attention, yet full scale models are typically too heavy for real time inference on devices such as the Raspberry Pi 5. This paper presents EdgeViT-SLAM, a lightweight hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for monocular visual odometry on edge platforms. The model combines depthwise separable convolutions with compact multi scale transformer blocks and a temporal spatial attention module, and is further optimized using knowledge distillation, structured pruning, and mixed precision quantization tailored to edge AI scenarios. We evaluate the system on standard visual odometry benchmarks, including the KITTI odometry and TUM RGB-D datasets, and conduct deployment experiments on a Raspberry Pi 5 platform. The results show that EdgeViT-SLAM achieves competitive trajectory accuracy compared to recent convolutional and transformer based baselines while significantly reducing model size and inference latency, making it suitable for real time robotic applications on low power edge hardware. © 2026 IEEE.},
