	abstract = {Time-of-Flight Diffraction (TOFD) is widely used for weld inspection, but reliable defect classification remains challenging because of strong noise, artefacts, and complex defect morphologies. To address these issues, we propose MCENet, a multi-scale cross-enhancement network that integrates convolutional feature extraction with an enhanced Transformer mechanism. A Local Feature Enhancement Module (LFEM) strengthens fine-grained structural information, while a lightweight Transformer module (LETM) generates cross tokens using depthwise separable convolutions and applies dual-softmax attention after downsampling to improve global representation with reduced computational cost. A dual-domain TOFD dataset comprising standard test blocks and engineering steel-structure welds was constructed for evaluation. MCENet achieved accuracies of 98.9% and 98.2% on the two domains, respectively, with 4.69 M parameters, 0.59 G FLOPs, and 33.6 ms edge inference latency. It also consistently outperformed the compared models across the reported metrics and showed reliable discrimination of high-risk planar defects, particularly cracks and lack of fusion. These results demonstrate that MCENet provides an effective balance between recognition accuracy and computational efficiency for practical TOFD weld-defect classification and subsequent structural safety assessment. © 2026 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
