	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048950231?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Edge devices such as NVIDIA Jetsons enable low-latency, privacy-preserving computation by processing data close to where it's captured, reducing round-Trip delays and keeping raw sensor streams on-device. CUDA kernels are especially important on these devices because they execute the core vision, control, and inference workloads directly on the GPU. But these devices require carefully tuned kernels to meet tight performance and energy envelopes, which is often performed manually and is a labor-intensive process. We present EdgeCudaCoder, an end-To-end, closed-loop multi-Agent framework that synthesizes, tests, deploys, and iteratively optimizes CUDA code directly on Jetson-class hardware. The system decomposes the development workflow across four agents-CodeWriter, TestWriter, CodeExecutor, and SelfReflection-that coordinate via natural-language directives and structured feedback. Beyond functional correctness, EdgeCuda-Coder integrates on-device energy measurements and power-mode controls to enforce user-specified constraints. © 2026 IEEE.},
