	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048958195?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {LLMs are increasingly used for on-device decision-making in domains such as code assistance, intelligent transportation, and robotics. Recent advances in model compression techniques, including quantization, have begun to reduce the compute and memory constraints of LLMs, enabling multi-billion parameter LLM inference on accelerated edge devices such as Jetson Orin and Thor. As applications deploy LLMs on edge devices, sandboxing their execution becomes increasingly important. In this work, we present a systematic benchmarking of LLM inference on two modern edge platforms, the NVIDIA Jetson Orin AGX and the NVIDIA Jetson Thor, across two sandboxed environments: Docker containers and WebAssembly (WASM), and contrast them with bare-metal execution. We evaluate four state-of-The-Art (SOTA) quantized LLMs spanning model sizes from 8B to 70B parameters, including Llama3.1, Gemma3, Qwen32B, and DeepSeek R1-Llama. Our evaluation focuses on key performance and resource overheads of sandboxing relevant to serverless-style edge execution, including inference latency, sensitivity to input sequence length, power and energy, and the performance impact relative to bare-metal execution. Our findings provide early insights for optimizing LLM inference on edge accelerators. © 2026 IEEE.},
