	doi = {10.1109/TASE.2026.3743062},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105054000734?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Pedestrian detection is a core perception task in intelligent transportation systems (ITS), especially for edge-deployed platforms that must operate under strict latency, memory, and power constraints. Although recent detectors have improved benchmark accuracy, many of them rely on deeper backbones, heavier feature fusion, or more complex attention designs, which limit their practical deployment on embedded platforms. To address this issue, this paper presents ZiLO, a lightweight, stackable, and scalable pedestrian detection framework for dense-scene perception on edge devices. Rather than introducing a new detection paradigm, ZiLO focuses on stage-aware computational reallocation through three coordinated designs: adaptive attention scheduling, dynamic channel scaling, and task-aligned residual connections. Experiments on CrowdHuman show that ZiLO achieves a competitive accuracy–efficiency trade-off. ZiLOBase reaches 0.8425 mAP@50 with only 1.69M parameters and 6.11 GFLOPs, while lighter variants further extend the design to more resource-constrained settings. In addi-tion, deployment on the AD-SHORTY autonomous platform with TensorRT FP16 sustains real-time performance above 111 FPS, and additional low-light and cross-dataset evaluations further support practical robustness. These results show that ZiLO provides an effective engineering-oriented solution for edge-side pedestrian detection in ITS scenarios. Our code is publicly available at: https://github.com/Rocky1salady-killer/ZiLO. © 2004 IEEE.},
	type = {Article},
