	doi = {10.1109/ICCSP68173.2026.11539670},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042269824?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Traditional sensors, such as GPS, barometric, and ultrasonic sensors, have limitations in accurately reflecting and dynamically capturing data from water surfaces, particularly during the unsafe landing of a UAV at precise locations. To overcome these limitations, a computer vision-driven landing zone detection framework using a YOLOv8 deep learning model is being trained on a custom dataset and imported within a MATLAB simulation environment. In this research, a MATLAB-based simulation architecture for identifying safe water landing zones in a camera-based virtual environment is designed. The custom datasets are annotated and trained using the YOLOv8 architecture with the best.pt weights are deployed in MATLAB for real-time inference. In the MATLAB environment, the virtual camera sensor processes input video streams, providing real-time object detection and classification and enabling the identification of safe landing zones over the water body under varying environmental conditions, including dynamic water and lighting conditions. The proposed system achieves high detection accuracy in safe-zone landing detection and demonstrates the effectiveness of the computer vision-based autonomous water-landing system for amphibious UAVs. The results show that the planned methods are suitable for applications, such as environmental monitoring, search and rescue, and maritime surveillance operations involving amphibious UAVs. Future work will focus on improving simulation-based validation for real-time embedded hardware deployment using an NVIDIA Jetson Nano, integrating an RGB camera and mmWave radar. © 2026 IEEE.},
