	abstract = {Uncontrolled screen exposure among children presents significant developmental and safety risks. While parental monitoring solutions exist, the vast majority rely on centralized collection of screenshots and application logs, raising severe privacy and security concerns. This paper presents Federated Screen Monitoring (FSM), a privacy-first, edge-intelligence framework that performs screen understanding entirely on the child's local device. FSM shares only localized model weight updates for collaborative learning, ensuring raw pixels never leave the edge. The system integrates an end-to-end pipeline utilizing deep learning-based EasyOCR for robust UI-text extraction, coupled with a lightweight TF-IDF and Logistic Regression classifier to categorize screen content into four distinct classes: Educational, Entertainment, Neutral, and Mixed. We evaluate FSM on a custom dataset of 340 realistic screenshots. The system achieves 92.3% OCR UI-text accuracy and an overall classification accuracy of 88.7%. Furthermore, we simulate an extended federated training environment using the FedAvg algorithm over 50 communication rounds with non-IID clients. The global model demonstrates strong convergence and robustness against data heterogeneity, improving accuracy seamlessly without transmitting a single screenshot. To validate the architectural choices, we conduct an extensive ablation study with rigorous statistical significance testing (p < 0.05). We also validate the system's feasibility through hardware profiling, demonstrating a memory footprint of under 220MB and inference latency below 200ms. FSM indicates that practical, highly accurate child screen monitoring can be achieved with low computational overhead while preserving absolute privacy by design. © 2026 IEEE.},
