	abstract = {Real-time classification of driver cognitive load is essential for driving safety, as cognitive load can impair attention, delay responses, and compromise decision-making during driving. Physiological signals can be used to estimate cognitive load, and their multimodal integration can improve robustness by exploiting complementary information across modalities. However, existing fusion models often rely on modality-specific encoder designs and hand-tuned hyperparameters, increasing architectural complexity and reducing efficiency in embedded environments. To address these limitations, this study presents a lightweight multimodal network with modality-consistent temporal encoding for real-time driver cognitive load classification on resource-constrained embedded devices. The proposed model employs a temporal convolutional network (TCN) to reduce complexity while effectively capturing temporal and multiscale features from heterogeneous physiological modalities. Adaptive average pooling and a lightweight attention module align and fuse the modality features, followed by a compact TCN and fully connected layers for the final classification. Experiments on the CL-Drive dataset demonstrated that a TCN-based feature extractor with a fixed kernel size can effectively capture the temporal characteristics of each modality without parameter tuning or network redesign. The proposed model achieved 86.22% accuracy in binary classification under 10-fold cross-validation, a 7.6% relative improvement over UniPhyNet, the state-of-the-art (SOTA) method, while minimizing parameters and floating-point operations by over 90%. On a Jetson Orin Nano (TensorRT FP32), it achieves a 2.3 ms inference latency, which is approximately 3.5 × faster than the SOTA method, demonstrating suitability for real-time driver-state monitoring on resource-constrained systems.  © 2013 IEEE.},
