	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.ast.2025.110823},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105016455161?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Optimizing the optimal specific impulse for Air Turbo Rocket (ATR) engines presents a non-convex optimization challenge, characterized by the presence of multiple local optima. Conventional global search algorithms, such as Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO), Genetic Algorithms, and Simulated Annealing can efficiently solve these problems. However, they often encounter a trade-off between stability and optimization speed. In recent years, artificial intelligence methods based on Reinforcement Learning (RL) have shown promising results in various applications. This study applies RL and introduces a novel phased multi-agent optimization method to enhance the optimization process in order to tackle the aforementioned challenges. The optimization procedure is divided into three phases. In the first and second phases, multi-agent RL with different speed constraints is employed to dynamically determine the velocity of each particle in the iteration process, yielding high global search capacities. In the third phase, a PSO algorithm featuring a low inertia coefficient is applied to enhance the optimization based on the results obtained in the second phase, which results in increasing the search precision. The proposed methodology is validated using 200 test cases within the full flight envelope of the ATR engine, conducted on an NVIDIA Jetson embedded computing module. The obtained results demonstrate that the proposed approach significantly increases the optimization stability. The worst-case optimization result across 100 repeated tests yields an 8.54 % improvement compared with conventional methods. In addition, the proposed method reduces the average number of iterations required to achieve the same optimization result by 49 %, while maintaining an average optimization time of 0.166 s per test case. © 2025 Elsevier Masson SAS},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Final},
