	abstract = {Global Navigation Satellite Systems (GNSS) play a vital role in delivering Positioning, Navigation, and Timing (PNT) services but remain highly vulnerable to intentional jamming, particularly in dynamic and challenging environments where conventional detection techniques are often ineffective. Recent advances in Artificial Intelligence (AI) enable more robust interference classification methods suitable for real-time operation. This work presents an optimized and lightweight Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) approach for GNSS jamming classification based on spectrogram representations, targeting low-latency edge deployments. The study explores systematic architectural optimizations, including spectrogram slicing, grayscale channel reduction, batch normalization, dropout regularization, quantization-aware training, and structured hyperparameter tuning, to reduce computational complexity while preserving discriminative spectral features. The resulting architecture demonstrates improved classification performance with a significantly reduced parameter footprint, while maintaining stable error characteristics across jamming classes. These results highlight the feasibility of deploying efficient AI-based GNSS interference detection on resource-constrained edge hardware, supporting reliable real-time operation in safety-critical applications.  © 2026 IEEE.},
