	doi = {10.1109/SCECS69812.2026.11565827},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044088174?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The rapid identification and evaluation of noncooperative aerial targets pose severe challenges for CounterUnmanned Aerial Systems (C-UAS) in complex environments. Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate significant potential in threat assessment; however, high computational costs, inference latency, and logical hallucinations strictly limit their real-time edge deployment. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a Neuro-Symbolic edge architecture utilizing quantized models. By decoupling deterministic hardware scoring from cognitive rationale generation, the framework bypasses the alignment tax and arithmetic bottlenecks inherent in lightweight models. Evaluations conducted on an edge testbed with 500 independent synthetic events indicate that the proposed architecture achieves a perfect threat assessment accuracy alongside a zero system crash rate within the evaluation boundary. Furthermore, the framework compresses end-to-end latency to 1.74 seconds for the two-billion parameter variant while maintaining an average peak power of 41.6 W. Expert reviews confirm 100% rationale faithfulness, proving the operational feasibility for real-time and safety-critical defense deployments in urban low-altitude security scenarios. © 2026 IEEE.},
