	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041686194?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Electric vehicle (EV) battery packs are susceptible to non-uniform heat which highly impacts safety, life, and energy efficiency. The traditional types of monitoring are based on point thermocouples that overlook localized hotspots and fast variation of temperatures. This paper presents an IoT-integrated thermal imaging system to detect potential anomalies in EV lithium-ion battery packs in real-time with the use of Edge AI models to map the temperature image accurately and measure the early signs of its potential development. An ESP32/Jetson Nano edge processor with a low-power FLIR Lepton 3.5 thermal sensor can be used to record high-resolution frames of temperature at 9Hz, and therefore can be used to do continuous monitoring without relying on the cloud. A thermal drift detection, hotspots, and abnormal temperature-rise prediction MobileNet CNN model is trained on an edge with a latency less than 80ms. The IoT dashboard is an MQTT system that delivers real-time temperature profiles, averaged temperature of the module, index of the hotspots zone and the forecasted thermal-runaway risk. When a 48 V EV battery module is loaded with drive-cycle loads and experimented, peak deviation of a hotspot is 12.6°C and 96.4 % of anomaly detection. The Communication overhead is also minimized 72 % with edge inference over cloud-based systems. This is a scalable, low-latency solution which can be deployed on two-wheelers, three-wheelers, and light electric vehicles to offer a safety-critical EV battery thermal health monitoring platform. © 2026 IEEE.},
