	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032176301?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The widespread application of artificial intelligence in food safety is driving the evolution of contaminant detection technologies toward greater efficiency and intelligence. Addressing the dual demands for efficiency and accuracy in food contaminant detection, this paper proposes an image recognition model that integrates an improved ResNet50 architecture with a multi-scale attention mechanism. Building upon traditional residual networks, the model incorporates lightweight convolutional structures alongside structural pruning and mixed-precision quantization techniques, significantly reducing computational overhead. To accommodate embedded deployment scenarios, the system designs an end-to-end rapid detection workflow integrated with CUDA inference acceleration and TensorRT optimization mechanisms. Experimental results demonstrate that on the Jetson AGX Orin platform, the improved model achieves an average recognition accuracy of 88.8% with a pruning rate of 0.4, and an inference latency of 68 ms. This outperforms MobileNetV3 (84.6%, 42 ms) and EfficientNet-B0 (86.1%, 51 ms). The model demonstrates robust stability and generalization capabilities across diverse pollutant types and complex edge geometries.  © 2026 SPIE.},
