	abstract = {Human action recognition plays a central role in applications such as surveillance, robotics, and human-computer interaction, where reliable recognition of human activities is essential. While deep learning has advanced recognition accuracy, most existing methods prioritize predictive performance while overlooking computational efficiency and deployability on embedded devices. This imbalance limits the translation of academic progress into real-Time systems where low latency and energy constraints are critical. In this paper, we present a complete pipeline for efficient HAR that addresses these limitations. We introduce an adaptive frame sampling strategy that ensures robust temporal coverage while reducing redundancy, and we adapt a pretrained V-JEPA backbone with an optimized classifier head to achieve strong recognition performance. To further improve efficiency, we apply mixed-precision quantization to computationally intensive layers, significantly accelerating inference with minimal accuracy loss. Finally, we deploy our models on the NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin, using TensorRT optimization and reporting throughput and latency benchmarks as reproducible references for real-world feasibility. Experiments on the UCF101 dataset show that our approach achieves 97.77% accuracy in full precision and maintains comparable performance under FP16/FP32 and INT8/FP32 quantization. On the Jetson AGX Orin, quantization improves throughput more than fourfold while reducing latency to real-Time levels. These results demonstrate that our pipeline not only delivers competitive accuracy but also establishes a practical benchmark for efficient, deployable action recognition systems.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
