	abstract = {Remote monitoring and teleoperation of robotic systems over the Internet require low-latency and bandwidth-efficient video transmission to ensure safe and responsive control. This article presents the development and experimental validation of a real-time video streaming architecture based on the WebRTC protocol, integrated into the ROS ecosystem using GStreamer. The proposed solution exploits hardware-accelerated video encoding on an NVIDIA Jetson Nano to optimize the transmission of RGB-D camera data over public networks. Connectivity across distinct networks and restrictive NAT and firewall environments is ensured through a TURN-assisted signaling mechanism. Experimental results evaluate glass-to-glass latency and bandwidth consumption, comparing the performance of H.264 and VP8 codecs at multiple video resolutions. The results demonstrate that hardware-accelerated H.264 significantly reduces bandwidth usage while maintaining low and stable latency suitable for real-time robotic teleoperation, whereas VP8 is only viable at low resolutions. Overall, the proposed architecture provides a feasible solution for remote robotic monitoring and control over public networks. © 2026 IEEE.},
