	abstract = {Thermal sensing plays an important role in automotive perception. Yet, most existing thermal datasets and thermal perception machine learning (ML) models are optimized for high-performance computing platforms. This work proposes a lightweight dual-attention fusion YOLO model designed for thermal imaging-based perception to run inference on edge devices. The proposed YOLO integrates efficient channel attention (ECA) and coordinate attention (CA) to enhance feature selectivity while maintaining compactness. The proposed model is evaluated on several thermal datasets, including ITDAV-25, OSU thermal, OSU color, FLIR ADAS, and C3I automotive thermal, which primarily target pedestrians and vehicles. To support practical deployment, a cloud-edge co-design strategy is introduced. All high-compute operations, such as data handling and training, are performed in the cloud, while the optimized model runs locally on the sensing device. This architecture reduces communication overhead, improves latency, and transforms thermal cameras into intelligent sensing nodes capable of autonomous operation. The trained model is benchmarked on several edge devices like Raspberry Pi 5, Rock Pi 4C+, Raspberry Pi Zero, and Jetson AGX Xavier to check the feasibility of real-time inference. The model is only 13.5MB in size, 6.59M parameters, and 15.3GFLOPS. Results show that the proposed model delivers improved detection accuracy over baseline YOLO variants like v5, v8, v9, v10, and v12 while preserving efficiency suitable for deployment in next-generation automotive sensor systems. © 2001-2012 IEEE.},
