	abstract = {Asymmetric underwater threats like naval mines and Improvised Explosive Devices (IEDs) are emerging threats to Indian Ocean Region (IOR) security. Acoustic sensors like sonar have limitations due to severe clutter in turbid waters; hence, a visual system to positively identify threats is required. However, due to high turbidity (Jerlov Type 9), optical sensors are not useful moreover, delays in cloud-based solutions are not suitable for autonomous response due to these delays. To address the aforementioned skillset deficit, the present paper proposes a novel architecture for an integrated native edge-computing-centric surveillance system to be employed within Autonomous Underwater Vehicles (AUVs) with limited capabilities. Our architecture takes a unique stance by diverging from traditional restoration-based AUVs to focus on semantic intelligibility to aid decision-making in AUVs. We propose a Quantum-Tensor Hybrid Architecture that leverages a Variational Quantum-Inspired circuit for haze removal alongside Tensor-Train Decomposition to minimize computational latency. We demonstrate the practical viability of our architecture on the NVIDIA Jetson Orin board with the AUV system registering an operational latency of less than 20 mss (78 FPS) alongside a 22 percent mAP improvement in detecting naval mines. © 2026 IEEE.},
