	doi = {10.1109/ICISCN67954.2026.11566395},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043699215?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Visually impaired people face huge barriers to autonomy in navigation, in part because of a limited awareness of the various dynamic obstructions, space topology, and the surrounding scene. The traditional assistive devices are also imperative alerts or sensor-based detection methods, which can be normally followed by cognitive overload, disjointed scene understanding and poor handling of time hazards. This research presents NAVQA, a multimodal Visual Question Answering framework specifically designed for assistive navigation. The system integrates real-time object detection using YOLOv8n and monocular depth estimation using MiDaS, together with ResNet-18-based localization against a pre-built knowledge base and graph-based path planning. At its core, NAVQA introduces a risk-modulated cross-modal transformer that dynamically scales attention weights using Time-to-Collision (TTC) priors and a reinforcement-learned adaptive query selector that jointly optimises semantic relevance, safety gain, and cognitive-load reduction. Unlike prior AVQA pipelines that rely on standard attention or generic reinforcement learning, our framework delivers explicit safety-aware fusion and edge-optimised query prioritisation. Deployed on NVIDIA Jetson Nano, it achieves $\mathbf{9 0. 3 \%}$ collision avoidance, $\mathbf{8 7. 2 \%}$ query relevance, 2.8% redundancy, and sub- 500 ms end-to-end latency. Ablation studies confirm that the risk-modulated attention and adaptive selection modules each contribute more than 8 % performance gains over baselines, establishing NAVQA as the first end-to-end, safetyprioritised multimodal VQA system for assistive navigation.  © 2026 IEEE.},
