	abstract = {Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML) is the general concept and field of ultra-low-power on-device machine learning, which enables resource-constrained edge devices to perform intelligent data processing locally. This capability is indispensable for sixth-generation (6G) networks. Unlike traditional approaches that rely on centralized or cloud-based processing, 6G requires distributed intelligence at the edge to meet its unprecedented demands for massive-scale device connectivity, ultrareliable low-latency communication (URLLC), and stringent energy and privacy constraints. Cloud-dependent machine learning cannot satisfy these requirements due to limitations in bandwidth, latency, and privacy. TinyML uniquely enables consumer electronics—such as wearables, smart home devices, and autonomous systems—to perform real-time adaptive inference locally, reducing dependence on network infrastructure and enhancing responsiveness and data security. Despite its promise, the broader adoption of TinyML in 6G is hindered by the scarcity of suitable datasets and standardized evaluation frameworks. This article presents a systematic analysis of publicly available datasets across key application domains, identifies critical gaps in data resources, and proposes benchmarks and guidelines to accelerate the development and evaluation of robust TinyML models for 6G-enabled consumer electronics. © 2026 IEEE.},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
