	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053893284?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) underpins Industry 4.0 by linking sensors, controllers, and analytics platforms into cyber-physical production systems, yet published surveys typically address single facets of the IIoT stack rather than providing a unified cross-layer perspective tailored to manufacturing. This paper presents a systematic survey grounded in a five-layer architectural framework—physical, network, middleware, service, and application—that classifies more than 70 technologies, protocols, and platforms against manufacturing-specific constraints including deterministic latency, EMI resilience, and 15–30 year equipment lifecycles. Following PRISMA guidelines, 1,847 records across five databases were screened to yield 247 primary studies (2014–2025). Nineteen quantitative comparison tables enable evidence-based technology selection, encompassing benchmarked edge-inference latencies, private-5G spectrum and deployment architectures, semantic-interoperability stacks, end-to-end latency budgets for four representative control loops, and a technology readiness assessment that ranks nineteen technologies on maturity, scalability, integration complexity, and cost, while a four-dimensional taxonomy organizes the literature by research objective, methodology, enabling technology, and application domain. Five industrial case studies (Siemens, Bosch, BMW, Foxconn, and an integrated steel plant) document real-world architectures, trade-offs, and quantified outcomes including 20–50% downtime reduction, 50% fewer quality escapes, and 5–15% energy savings. Per-layer lessons learned, six interconnected challenge groups, five cross-layer design principles, and a research roadmap toward Industry 5.0 provide actionable guidance for both researchers and practitioners architecting smart-factory IoT deployments. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
