	abstract = {Illegal construction vehicles (e.g., excavators, cranes) around transmission lines are the main cause of external damage faults. Existing edge detection schemes either have excessive power consumption (5-15W for Jetson platforms) or suffer from insufficient accuracy or real-time performance due to improper model compression. This paper proposes a hardware-constrained cascaded compression paradigm for STM32H7 series microcontrollers, which integrates knowledge distillation, channel pruning, INT8 quantization and hardware-aware neural architecture search (HA-NAS) to jointly optimize the MobileNetV2-SSD detection model. Experimental results show that the optimized model size is 980KB, which can be fully stored in on-chip Flash (2MB). It achieves 78ms inference latency at 480 \mathrm{MHz}, 0.28 \mathrm{~W} average power consumption, and 90.3 \% mAP on the self-built dataset and 87.1% mAP on the public dataset. Extensive ablation experiments verify the optimality of the proposed cascaded compression sequence. This work systematically solves the model-hardware matching and accuracy-power trade-off problems, providing an engineering-feasible low-power solution for fixed monitoring nodes of transmission line anti-external damage. © 2026 IEEE.},
