	abstract = {Rapid urbanization has placed unprecedented strain on transportation systems, intensifying interactions between motor vehicles and vulnerable road users and leading to increased accidents, congestion, and safety risks. However, existing safety solutions rely heavily on cloud servers or dense roadside infrastructure, which introduce latency, privacy risks, and high deployment costs. Meanwhile, edge devices face tight limits on flash memory, RAM, and power to execute advanced machine-learning models in real time. To address these limitations, we propose SafePath, a low-power edge AI framework for real-time vulnerable road user protection. SafePath performs fully on-device inference by fusing camera and inertial measurement unit (IMU) data using lightweight neural models. The framework integrates compact convolutional neural networks for visual detection, a Tiny U-Net for segmentation, small audio keyword-spotting models, and efficient motion classifiers to detect and respond to imminent road hazards. All models are optimized through quantization, pruning, and knowledge distillation to fit within microcontroller constraints, requiring less than 2 MB of flash memory, minimal RAM, and under 100 mW of power. We also introduce a budget-aware deployment heuristic that selects sensor locations to minimize pedestrian and cyclist risk under cost limits. Experiments on the Cityscapes dataset, field simulations, and prototype deployments show over 99% hazard detection accuracy with end-to-end inference latency below 50 ms on ARM Cortex-M hardware. Overall, SafePath provides an efficient next-generation Safety-as-a-Service (SaafaS) infrastructure to protect vulnerable road users. © 2026 Published by Elsevier B.V.},
