	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043756127?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The Crop disease greatly decreases agricultural output especially where farmers use low-end mobile devices and have no uninterrupted internet connectivity. Although recent state-of-the-art deep learning methods have proved to show encouraging outcomes on crop disease detection, the majority of current solutions rely on cloud-based inference, costly hardware, or do not provide safety-conscious treatment recommendations. This paper introduces AR-CropShield, which is an offline, lightweight Android-based implementation of the real-time crop disease detection and confidence-reliant precision treatment guidance. The proposed system employs a lightweight convolutional neural network that is optimized to run on TensorFlow Lite to carry out on-devices inferences on Android 8 and up low-end smartphones. A camera-based augmented reality (AR) interface is provided in the form of a small-scale device that shows disease information and instructions on how to treat the crop on top of the live view of the crop to increase usability in the real-world. One of the major contributions in this piece is the development of a confidence-based treatment recommendation system, which combines model confidence scores with expert-approved agricultural recommendations to reduce excessive-prescription of pesticides in addition to boosting the safety of farmers. The system is designed as decision-support tool rather than a diagnostic authority, so all of the treatment recommendations are based on the practices recommended by International Center for Scientific Agricultural Research (ICSAR). The present paper describes the system architecture, treatment methodology, and a proposed evaluation protocol, with respect to accuracy of about 98%, inference latency and performance on low-end mobile devices. AR-CropShield seeks to show that effective, safe, affordable, and precision agriculture tools could be used completely offline with the help of consumer smartphones.  © 2026 IEEE.},
