	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/LSENS.2026.3735933},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051583425?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Preimpact fall (PIF) detection aims to recognize fall-related motions within the short interval before body impact, requiring both reliable early recognition and efficient on-device inference. However, existing deep learning methods often incur high latency due to complex temporal feature extraction. This letter presents stimulus-triggered internal recovery filter (STIR), a bio-inspired stimulus-triggered internal recovery filter for lightweight inertial measurement unit (IMU)-based PIF detection. STIR generates temporal response features through acceleration stimuli and internal state recovery, which are then fed into a lightweight MLP classifier for recognition. On the SisFall dataset, the proposed STIR-based model achieves 97.72% test accuracy with only 26.3 k floating-point operations (FLOPs). Compared with the lightweight one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1DCNN) baseline, STIR reduces FLOPs by 97.0% and inference latency by 91.3%. On the STM32U575RIT6 microcontroller unit (MCU), it achieves an average inference time of 2.6 ms with an estimated energy consumption of 26.32 μJ, while requiring only 2.56 KB of peak random-access memory (RAM) and 49.29 KB of flash storage. These results suggest that STIR has potential for real-time IMU-based PIF detection on resource-constrained wearable devices.  © 2017 IEEE.},
