	abstract = {Sparse rewards and limited onboard computation pose major challenges for multi-unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) cooperative search. Existing multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) methods often rely on computationally heavy exploratory networks or employ rigid exploration strategies that fail to adapt dynamically. To address these practical bottlenecks, this paper proposes Ada-Dual, a lightweight edge-intelligent MARL framework. First, we construct dual potential fields to provide structured spatial guidance, which are asynchronously updated via low-bandwidth telemetry to suit communication-constrained environments. Second, to regulate the exploration-exploitation balance under sparse feedback, a lightweight arbitration variable is embedded into the policy output. This enables an autonomous adaptation of the exploration ratio across varying task densities, mitigating the limitations of static exploration weights under distribution shifts. A gradient-blocking mechanism is further employed to decouple this arbitration optimization from high-variance extrinsic rewards, improving training stability. Hardware-in-the-loop tests on an NVIDIA Jetson Xavier NX demonstrate a real-time inference speed of 125.3 FPS using a highly compact policy network. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that Ada-Dual achieves highly competitive and stable performance across heterogeneous scenarios. By balancing adaptability and computational efficiency, the proposed framework offers a practical, low-overhead alternative to computationally expensive exploration methods for edge-deployed UAV swarms. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
	type = {Article},
