	abstract = {With 3D point cloud processing widely used in autonomous driving, robotics, and industrial inspection, deploying highperformance classification models on resource-constrained edge devices remains a critical bottleneck. Recent highperformance models such as PointMLP, PointTransformerV2 and PointMamba achieve over 94% accuracy on ModelNet40, but rely on heavy architectures with millions of parameters, making them difficult to deploy on edge devices.This paper proposes CurveNetLite, a lightweight network designed for edge deployment, addressing the large parameter counts and high computational costs of existing models like CurveNet. Through the co-design of a Progressive Channel Reduction (PCR) strategy and an Adaptive Curve Number (ACN) mechanism, CurveNetLite significantly reduces parameters and computational complexity while preserving core feature extraction capabilities. Experimental results on ModelNet40 show parameters reduced from 1.2M to 0.68M (43% reduction) with only 0.49% accuracy drop (93.4%→92.91%), achieving an excellent performance-efficiency trade-off. Cross-validation on ModelNet10 confirms strong generalization (accuracy loss %). Compared to PointNet++, DGCNN, and PointNeXt, CurveNetLite achieves the optimal parameter/accuracy ratio (7,323) and reduces inference latency to 28ms on edge hardware, providing an efficient solution for edge-based 3D point cloud classification.  © 2026 SPIE.},
