	abstract = {This paper investigates how effective We- bAssembly 3.0 paired with the WebAssembly System Interface (WASI) could serve as a practical runtime for safety-critical edge deployments that demand low latency, portability, and controlled access to system resources. We do this by benchmarking a curated set of representative algorithms across a variety of execution environments, including a Raspberry Pi-based edge node and Vercel. In addition, the paper also explains the working of WebAssembly and how it was designed to be language and platform independent and how WASI exposes OS-dependant services using a capability-based security model that limits filesystem, networking, and other privileged operations to explicitly granted resources. The results find that compared to Docker, WASI reduces peak memory consumption by 49.2 % requiring only 18.33 MB on average, while still executing most safety-critical algorithms in under a millisecond and WASI provides measurable performance and deployment benefits for certain edge- style workloads, and faces some trade-offs compared to conventional container-based approaches in others. Finally, we discuss practical implications, summarizing the benefits and demerits of edge computing, Raspberry Pi-class devices, and WASI-based runtimes, and outline when such a switch is an appropriate choice for safety-critical edge systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
