	abstract = {Safety monitoring systems for workers in industrial environments typically use fixed, population-wide thresholds to detect unsafe physiological and behavioral states, often ignoring inter-individual differences in posture, heart rate, and movement patterns. Such limitation may lead to more false alarms and less reliability in real industrial environments. In this paper, a Context-Aware Personalized Safety Model is proposed. The model builds an individual physiological and behavioral baseline for each worker through an initial personalized calibration phase. The personalized baseline is dynamically adjusted according to contextual information such as activity type and environmental conditions to enhance the accuracy of the risk assessment. This paper uses a lightweight rolling-window anomaly detection algorithm to analyze real-time sensor data for deployment on an ESP32 sensor node with Raspberry Pi 5 edge inference for low-latency, privacy-preserving worker monitoring. The approach was validated on activity recordings of nine subjects from the PAMAP2 dataset and its performance was assessed in terms of accuracy, precision, recall and F1-score. Experimental results show that the proposed model achieves an overall accuracy, precision, recall and F1-score of 99.40%, respectively, which demonstrates the effectiveness of the proposed model in recognizing worker activities accurately and enables context-aware personalized safety monitoring. The results show that the combination of personalized calibration and Edge AI inference offers a feasible and scalable solution for intelligent industrial worker safety in Industrial IoT scenarios. © 2026 IEEE.},
