	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105029074644?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {To address the issue of low ball pickup efficiency in pickleball training and competitions, this paper presents an embedded multi-target detection system based on YOLOv5 and ROS. The system utilizes Jetson Nano and Orbbec Gemini Pro cameras as the hardware platform, with the lightweight YOLOv5 model deployed in a Conda virtual environment. A resource-aware node scheduling and dual-format input compatibility scheme is proposed, enabling on-demand processing and dynamic switching between compressed and uncompressed images, effectively reducing the computational load. The model, trained on a single-class pickleball DS, achieves Precision = 0.951, Recall = 0.957, andmAP@0.5 = 0.987 on the validation set. Experimental results demonstrate that the system offers high accuracy, low latency, and robust performance on embedded platforms, providing a feasible solution for the efficient deployment of visual perception in sports robots.  © COPYRIGHT SPIE. Downloading of the abstract is permitted for personal use only.},
