	doi = {10.1109/ISOIRS70157.2026.11545249},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042421487?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Vehicle target detection is a core perceptual component of autonomous driving, directly influencing the safety and reliability of intelligent driving systems. Despite advancements in detection techniques, challenges like imbalanced road target distribution, frequent occlusion of small/distant objects, and environmental interference persist, degrading accuracy and real-time performance in dynamic driving environments. To address these issues, this study optimizes YOLOv10 for real-time vehicle detection in intelligent driving scenarios and proposes three key strategies: (1) FASFFHead, an adaptive cross-scale feature fusion detection head to enhance multi-scale target adaptability; (2) MSDA (Multi-Dilation-Rate Self-Attention) mechanism to improve robustness in complex scenes (e.g., occlusion, low visibility); (3) RepNCSPELAN4_UIB, a lightweight adaptive feature propagation module for efficient on-board deployment. Experiments validate the model’ s performance: on the BDD100K dataset, the improved ML-YOLOv10 achieves a ~7.2% increase in small-target recall, a ~4.1% improvement in large-target precision, and an overall mAP@0.5 of 92.3% (~3.8% higher than the baseline YOLOv10). Meanwhile, it reduces parameters by ~19.5% (12.8M→10.3M) while maintaining real-time inference (68 FPS on NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX). Cross-dataset tests on KITTI confirm a mAP@0.5 of 89.7% (3.2% higher than YOLOv10), and real-vehicle road tests in urban/highway scenarios achieve an average detection accuracy of 91.4%. These results demonstrate that the proposed optimization strategies effectively address the core challenges of intelligent driving target detection, balancing accuracy, lightweight design, and real-time performance for practical on-board deployment. © 2026 IEEE.},
