	doi = {10.1109/LRA.2026.3746147},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105054777108?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Liquid-to-gas phase-change actuators provide compact, compliant actuation, but their nonlinear pressure dynamics, thermal delay, and asymmetric heating-cooling response make precise control difficult. Building on an established Physical Hysteretic ReservoirComputing (PhysRes) architecture, this letter integrates a two-model PhysRes predictor with sampling-based Model Predictive Control (MPC) and deploys it on an edge platform. Separately trained scalar pressure and joint two-output temperature models use shifted-sigmoid reservoirs, with only their linear readouts fitted by closed-form ridge regression. With 1,050 training samples, PhysRes predicts chamber pressure, Peltier temperature, and liquid temperature with R<sup>2</sup> ≥ 0.9977 for each state. Among all evaluated predictors, PhysRes achieves the lowest measured desktop one-step inference latency of 0.013ms, 12.0× faster than a fully trained long short-term memory (FT-LSTM) network. Across experiments at 120, 130, and 140kPa, PhysRes-MPC achieves a mean absolute error (MAE) of 1.95kPa, 32.2% lower than proportional-integral-derivative (PID) control, and the lowest maximum overshoot at each target. On the ESP32, frozen PhysRes and FT-LSTM pressure predictors require 157.0 and 2090.2µs per inference, respectively; their corresponding 200-candidate, 10-step rollouts require 316.5 and 4205.5ms. Our source code and data are available at https://github.com/KawaharaLab/physres-mpc. © 2016 IEEE.},
