	abstract = {Interactive vision applications such as extended reality (XR) and robotics rely on instant and comprehensive perception to maintain seamless user experiences. However, real-time mobile vision systems struggle to sustain low-latency inference as object vocabularies grow, leading to increased model footprints and inflated latency. We present MERCI, a runtime system for class-specific video inference that exploits class sparsity, where the number of classes active at any moment is often limited; fewer than 3% of 638 classes in EgoObjects. MERCI utilizes a two-tier adaptation design to mitigate the coverage gaps and retraining delays. At runtime, a greedy multi-expert composition algorithm selects specialized models from a model zoo to cover active class sets. When composition becomes inefficient, a similarity-driven integration algorithm leverages existing experts to build compact models with fast convergence. Extensive experiments across multiple datasets and Jetson platforms demonstrate that MERCI enhances streaming accuracy by up to 26.96% compared to direct integration baselines.  © 2026 IEEE.},
