	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036955495?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Reliable human-drone interaction represents a critical enabling technology for the operation of Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) in complex, unstructured environments. However, conventional vision-based gesture control systems often prove inadequate in practical settings, where their accuracy degrades due to unpredictable background clutter, inherent sensor noise, and temporal misalignments within gesture sequences, collectively posing risks to operational safety. To overcome these limitations, we present MM-GRNet - a Multi-Modal Spatio-Temporal Attention Network that establishes a new paradigm for robust UAV gesture interaction. Our approach systematically integrates an Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) for real-time denoising of skeletal trajectories, a computationally efficient dual-stream backbone leveraging parallel 1D-CNN and Bi-LSTM pathways for joint spatial-temporal representation learning, and a trainable attention mechanism that adaptively focuses on the most discriminative segments of a gesture. Confronted with the prohibitive costs and risks of collecting anomalous flight data from physical platforms, we constructed a physics-based synthetic dataset to rigorously evaluate model performance. MM-GRNet attains a recognition accuracy of 95.2% on this benchmark, outperforming existing methods by a significant margin. When deployed on an NVIDIA Jetson Nano edge platform, the system maintains a mean inference latency of 12ms, meeting the stringent real-time demands of in-flight UAV control. © 2026 IEEE.},
