	abstract = {Industry 5.0 envisions a human-centric, cognitively aware transportation ecosystem emphasizing resilience, personalization, and sustainable human-machine collaboration. This paper proposes a unified cognitive edge intelligence framework that transforms individual driving behavior into collective, predictive safety intelligence. The framework integrates Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML), cooperative perception, and human-in-the-loop augmented intelligence for real-time driver behavior understanding at the network edge. Unlike traditional cloud-centric or reactive systems, our approach employs lightweight, optimized TinyML models on in-vehicle edge units to infer behavioral states, normal, slow, and aggressive, directly from inertial sensor streams with minimal latency. This cognitive state is shared via an Internet of Vehicles (IoV) layer, enabling collective risk anticipation beyond line-of-sight. A digital shadow aggregates behavioral insights from connected vehicles to support long-term trend analysis and statistical anomaly detection, completing a closed-loop intelligence cycle from edge inference to strategic fleet oversight. The framework is experimentally validated across multiple dimensions. Real-world driving data demonstrates the system's efficacy, achieving 97.75% test accuracy and 95.7% accuracy on a separate real-world deployment dataset, with an ultralight model size of 0.03 MB and a per-inference latency of 0.35 ms. The cooperative Vehicle-to-Vehicle (V2V) mechanism achieves a Packet Delivery Ratio (PDR) exceeding 96.5% with end-to-end latency below 2 ms across highway traffic densities, while the digital shadow enables behavioral trend monitoring and statistical anomaly detection. These results demonstrate that the proposed framework delivers both immediate roadside safety and long-term system-wide resilience for next-generation transportation systems. © 1967-2012 IEEE.},
