	doi = {10.1016/j.tre.2026.105274},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053885854?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Large language models (LLM) are driving the deep integration of artificial intelligence and intelligent shipping, ushering in a new technological paradigm. Although existing deep learning–based ship trajectory prediction methods have achieved significant progress, their capacity to integrate non-numerical semantic knowledge remains insufficient, yet such knowledge is crucial for accurately characterizing ship behavior patterns. To bridge this gap, we propose LLM-EdgeSTP, the first LLM-empowered and edge-deployable ship trajectory prediction framework that unifies continuous automatic identification system (AIS) trajectories with discrete maritime textual knowledge through cross-modal reasoning. The method comprises three core components. (1) The maritime semantic prompt unit (MSPU) transforms raw AIS trajectories into context-aware natural language prompts. (2) The navigational interaction representation unit (NIRU) models ship–ship interactions by fusing convolutional and attention mechanisms and maps them into the LLM embedding space through representation–language reconstruction to enable semantic understanding of encounter and avoidance intentions. Lightweight LLMs such as LLaMA and Gemma then fuse navigational states and interaction semantics to generate trajectory predictions in natural language form and decode them into precise coordinates. (3) Edge deployment supports real-time inference on low-power hardware. Experiments across three representative maritime regions demonstrate that LLM-EdgeSTP outperforms ten baseline models by an average of 40% in terms of average displacement error (ADE) and final displacement error (FDE) while satisfying real-time latency requirements. This work lays a key technical foundation for building interpretable, efficient, and practical autonomous navigation systems. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
