	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/CAIT70489.2026.11553843},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042871998?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The increasing accessibility of Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) raises safety concerns regarding national public safety and the protection of infrastructure, necessitating reliable detection of low, slow, and small (LSS) targets. However, existing vision-based approaches face multiple structural and environmental challenges when detecting such miniature objects, such as severe background clutter and high computational redundancy that often impede deployment on edge devices. To this end, we propose FSA-Net, a lightweight Frequency-Enhanced Spatial Attention Network. Targeting the interference from high-frequency background clutter, we introduce a frequency-domain enhanced convolutional block attention module that leverages a per-channel standard deviation pooling stream to separate UAV signatures from natural backgrounds. Against feature vanishing at sub-pixel scales usually caused by conventional image down-sampling, we propose a high-resolution dynamic slicing strategy that actively preserves the absolute spatial fidelity of tiny targets. To adhere to the restrictive computational limits inherent to embedded edge memory configurations, the entire framework is structurally anchored upon an optimized ResNet-18 backbone coupled with a lightweight Feature Pyramid Network, striking a favorable balance between network lightness and recognition accuracy. Extensive experiments on the Det-Fly dataset demonstrate that the proposed method achieves an F1-score of 88.4% and a recall of 91.3%, outperforming several established larger baseline models. Evaluated for edge deployment, the unified framework attains real-time inference at 28 FPS with a maximum power consumption of 5 W, confirming its practical viability for embedded counter-UAV security platforms.  © 2026 IEEE.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
