	abstract = {The rapid expansion of connected and autonomous vehicles has intensified the demand for intelligent, ultra-low-latency, and energy-efficient processing architectures capable of handling massive multimodal data streams in real time. Conventional cloud-centric vehicular intelligence frameworks suffer from excessive latency, bandwidth congestion, and privacy concerns, limiting their applicability in safety-critical scenarios. The current paper introduces a next generation edge assisted vehicular intelligence system, namely, Neuro-Diffusion Graph Spiking Digital-Twin Network (NDGSDT-Net) that combines self-supervised representation learning, spatio-temporal graph neural network, continuous-time Neural ODE modeling, spiking neural network based neuromorphic inference, diffusion-guided rare event generation and digital twin simulation in a cooperative edge system. The suggested architecture allows real-time perception, predictive reasoning, and decision-making and keeps a high level of energy and latency requirements. Large-scale experimental analyses indicate that NDGSDT-Net has an accuracy of 97.3%, F1-score of 96.9%, and recall of 96.6%, which are superior to the current deep learning and hybrid baselines. The edge inference latency was kept at about 22ms and energy consumption is 129mJ per inference, which further confirms its suitability to large-scale implementation. The findings affirm that combination of rare learning paradigms with the help of synergy, essentially enhances the reliability, robustness and efficiency of vehicle intelligence. The present work provides an incremental basis of scalably cooperative, privacy-compliant, and explainable intelligent transportation systems in the future.  © 2026 IEEE.},
