	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.neucom.2026.133585},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034976706?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The Bird's-Eye-View (BEV) paradigm has become a foundational framework for multi-view 3D object detection in autonomous driving. However, current sparse query-based methods still struggle to effectively integrate 2D prior information, facing persistent challenges such as inefficient backbone networks, inaccurate depth estimation, unreliable query initialization, and error propagation from 2D detection. To address these challenges, we propose the Depth-Aware Sparse Queries (DASQ) framework. Supported by an efficient backbone network, DASQ first fuses probabilistic and regressed depth estimates to enhance depth accuracy, then converts filtered 2D detections into 3D prior queries, and finally integrates them into the global BEV representation via an attention mechanism. This approach significantly boosts both the accuracy and inference efficiency of BEV perception. In evaluations on the nuScenes benchmark, DASQ achieves a 3.2% improvement in mAP while attaining real-time inference at 10.1 FPS on an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin platform, demonstrating its strong potential for practical autonomous driving applications. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
