	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037450998?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Modern Cyber-Physical Systems, such as autonomous vehicles and industrial robots, require real-time execution of AI models directly at the network edge. Cloud processing introduces critical latency and standard CPUs are insufficient for these computational demands, making on-device 'Edge AI' essential. This paper comparatively analyzes the primary hardware accelerators: Graphics Processing Units, Field-Programmable Gate Arrays, and dedicated Neural Processing Units. We evaluate these architectures against key CPS metrics: performance-per-watt, inference latency, development complexity, and reconfigurability. The analysis shows GPUs offer high throughput but consume significant power. FPGAs provide superior flexibility and low latency for custom algorithms but require significant development effort. VPUs deliver exceptional energy efficiency for common neural networks but lack flexibility. The findings conclude that no single solution is optimal. The selection is a critical engineering trade-off determined by the CPS application's specific constraints on power, cost, and development time.  © 2026 IEEE.},
