	abstract = {This study presents XFG-SLAM, a deep learning–based visual SLAM system designed for real-time operation on low-power edge devices. Unlike conventional SLAM systems that rely on handcrafted visual features—often resulting in limited robustness and inconsistent performance under challenging visual conditions—XFG-SLAM introduces a system-level design that enables learned visual features to be deployed efficiently under strict computational and energy constraints. The proposed framework integrates a lightweight deep feature extractor with a fully GPU-resident, CUDA-accelerated processing pipeline, employing task-level parallelism and stream-based execution to minimize end-to-end latency. Built upon the ORB-SLAM3 backbone, XFG-SLAM replaces the traditional ORB feature stage with XFeat while preserving compatibility with classical SLAM modules. Experimental evaluations on the TUM RGB-D benchmark demonstrate that XFG-SLAM achieves tracking accuracy comparable to ORB-SLAM3 while operating at nearly 20 frames per second on a 7 W embedded GPU, with improved runtime consistency across multiple sequences. These results establish XFG-SLAM as a practical and deployable framework for real-time deep feature–based SLAM on battery-powered embedded robotic platforms, supporting applications such as lightweight real-time 3D environment scanning, autonomous navigation, and field inspection. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
