	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048975053?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Quantised Small Language Models (SLMs) deployed at the edge are increasingly relevant for enabling conversational agents in interactive virtual worlds, where low-latency inference is important for maintaining responsive and seamless interaction. This paper investigates the deployment of quantised SLMs at the edge to examine the runtime performance requirements and response quality for real-time conversational interaction in an immersive virtual world. We developed a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) back-end system to support a guide agent in virtual museum and evaluated two SLM families with three quantisation levels (8-bit, 4-bit, and 3-bit) on an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX. Performance was assessed through end-to-end latency, time-to-first-token, throughput, and memory headroom, and response quality was examined through expert ratings of accuracy, relevance, safety, and human alignment. Results show that moderate quantisation enables performant practical edge deployment with good response quality, demonstrating that edge-deployed quantised SLMs can support real-time narrative interactions and remain practically useful for virtual-world agents, providing evidence for deploying high performance conversational AI in immersive environments. © 2026 IEEE.},
