	abstract = {Child-centered social robots must be responsive, safe, and aligned with family expectations. While large language models (LLMs) improve conversational fluency, prompt-only control is insufficient for reliable child interaction because it does not explicitly encode behavioral state, parental constraints, and runtime safety checks. This study presents a real-time architecture that combines multimodal perception (attention, engagement, and facial affect trends) with a parent-configured child profile and an event-driven LLM controller on edge hardware (NVIDIA Jetson Xavier). We formalize a unified contextual state as both a numerical state vector and a structured JSON schema used by the dialogue policy layer. We also implement explicit post-generation validation rules to enforce hard parental constraints before robot actuation. Quantitative system evaluation reports throughput, end-to-end latency decomposition, and robustness under noisy speech and perception dropouts. Results show that Qwen 2.5 (3B) provides the best real-time trade-off on-device, and that structured constraint enforcement improves policy compliance during stress tests. The proposed architecture provides a practical and interpretable foundation for culturally aligned Arabic language interaction, while controlled child outcome studies are defined as the next evaluation step. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
