	doi = {10.1109/LSENS.2026.3736534},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051693975?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Ensuring the correct use of personal protective equipment (PPE) is essential for occupational safety. However, maintaining continuous compliance monitoring remains challenging, particularly in environments with limited infrastructure. This letter presents a low-cost embedded visual sensing system for real-time safety helmet detection based on Tiny Machine Learning (TinyML), integrating an image sensor with a resource-constrained ESP32-S3-EYE microcontroller to perform fully on-device inference. The proposed approach employs a lightweight FOMO model with a MobileNetV2 backbone. Model optimization through input resolution selection, TensorFlow Lite conversion, and post-training quantization reduced the model size from 36 MB to 55 kB, with the final deployed model achieving a precision of 71%, recall of 74%, and F1-score of 73%. The embedded implementation achieved an average neural network inference latency of 10.6 ms per image (approximately 94 FPS), while the complete application cycle required approximately 5 s. The proposed system enables autonomous PPE monitoring without relying on cloud infrastructure, thereby reducing latency, preserving data privacy, and increasing deployment flexibility. These results demonstrate that TinyML-based embedded vision can provide a practical and scalable solution for low-cost occupational safety monitoring. © 2026 IEEE. All rights reserved.},
