	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049050167?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper describes an AI-based digital security management system for schoolchildren. The proposed system comprises three modules: an NLP threat detector (DistilBERT, 0.92 precision, 0.89 recall for cyberbullying detection), a behavioral anomaly detector (Isolation Forest, AUC = 0.94), and a real-time intervention agent with on-device inference. A two-week pilot deployment was conducted in two secondary schools (n = 210 students, 48 000 sessions). The system achieved an average latency of 187 ms (95th percentile = 214 ms) and a false positive rate of 4.1%. An ablation study demonstrated that combining textual and behavioral modalities increased recall by 17 percentage points over a text-only baseline. The architecture balances detection performance, real-time constraints, and differential privacy guarantees (ϵ = 2.1), confirming that AI-driven security can be deployed in K-12 environments without degrading student experience or increasing teacher workload. © 2026 IEEE.},
