	abstract = {This research presents a compact, real-time LiDAR (Light Detection and Ranging)-based safety architecture that is simple to include into conventional four-wheeled vehicles. Situational awareness and vehicle safety are enhanced by the technology through trustworthy point-cloud visualization and trustworthy perception in bad weather. Unlike typical LiDAR implementations that deteriorate in heavy rain, fog, and dust, the proposed technique maintains dependability through adaptive filtering and efficient LiDAR processing. ROS2 is used on an NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano to create a three-stage pipeline for low-latency edge computation: voxel-grid downsampling, RANSAC ground segmentation, and statistical outlier removal. Experimental evaluation in a range of road settings shows an average processing delay of less than 100 ms, a detection accuracy of 91.7%, and an 8.6% false-positive rate within a 30 m range. The results demonstrate a scalable, low-cost platform that may be applied to both personal and commercial vehicle applications.  © 2026 IEEE.},
