	abstract = {For safe operation in resource-constrained and changing environments, selfdriving cars in Internet of Vehicles (IoV) networks require accurate and effective perception systems. However, under poor environmental conditions such as” different light, interference by weather, and occlusion of scenes, traditional vision-based models often fall short performing below par. Here, spatiotemporal attention-enhanced convolutional neural network (CNN)–bidirectional long short-term memory (BiLSTM) is introduced that combines attention-based refinement, bidirectional temporal context modeling, and spatial feature extraction. For improved motion understanding, the BiLSTM extracts forward and backward temporal relations, and the CNN module pools dense visual features of adjacent frames. These are followed by a multihead spatial attention mechanism that identifies salient regions, i.e., traffic lanes and moving objects. A series of tests were performed using an NVIDIA Jetson Xavier edge computer on the BDD100K and KITTI datasets in a simulated IoV environment. Following optimization, the suggested model achieved a model size of 48 MB, real-time inference speed of 26 frames per second, lane detection intersection over union of 84.7%, F1 score of 91.3%, object detection mean average precision (mAP)@0.5 of 78.6%, and mAP@0.75 of 69.2%. These findings indicate that the model is superior to current baselines in terms of accuracy, computational efficiency, and temporal robustness and thus could be used in real-world autonomous IoV applications. © 2026 Scrivener Publishing LLC.},
