	abstract = {Emergency vehicle (EV) siren recognition is a safety-critical audio task where robust and transferable Machine Learning (ML) solutions remain limited by dataset weak labels, acoustic variability, and edge-deployment constraints. This paper presents a systematic study of General-Purpose Audio Tagging (GP-AT) models adaptation for the EV recognition downstream domain. We first profile representative pre-trained architectures to identify efficient candidates, then extend the data dimension of the problem through three complementary resources: AudioSet-EV v2, AudioSet-EV Strong, and KineScaper-EV, supporting, respectively, larger-scale and real-world clip-level classification, detection-oriented evaluation, and controlled robustness analysis. Fine-tuning from AudioSet initialization on AudioSet-EV v2 yields the most effective transferable binary setting, with E-PANNs attaining the best overall accuracy/efficiency cross-dataset trade-off). Under unified real-world and synthetic training, CLAP achieves the strongest cross-dataset precision, whereas E-PANNs remains the most practical solution for window-based detection. Overall, the results show that dataset content quality, annotation reliability, and controlled variability are as critical as architecture and training design for deployable safety machine listening. © 2025 IEEE.},
