	abstract = {Recognizing fear in real-time improves personal safety in dangerous situations. This paper presents Star Sentinel, a real-time wearable multimodal fear detection and alert system. Star Sentinel integrates heart rate variability and speech features, extracts compact on-device feature vectors, and runs a lightweight neural classifier optimized for low-latency inference on resource-constrained hardware. A functional wearable prototype is presented, along with test-set results achieving 94% accuracy, with precision, recall, and F1-scores of approximately 80%, and an Area Under the Curve greater than 0.98. End-to-end latency and model size are measured to confirm real-time operation. Comparative experiments using heart rate variability-only, speech-only, and fused features indicate that the fused classifier outperforms heart rate variability-only baselines, while performance degrades significantly when speech features are removed or corrupted. Key engineering trade-offs, deployment constraints, and privacy considerations are also examined. © 2026 IEEE.},
