	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053695294?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This article presents a robust Adaptive Continuous Higher-Order Sliding Mode Control (ACHOSMC) framework augmented with a Dual Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) architecture for the integrated guidance and control (IGC) of autonomous aerospace vehicles. Unlike traditional static-gain methods, the proposed system employs two independently trained LSTM networks to dynamically schedule the boundary-layer and switching gains in real-time. This dual-channel adaptation enables the controller to proactively compensate for severe aerodynamic nonlinearities and stochastic disturbances while effectively suppressing chattering. Theoretical stability is rigorously established via an Input-to-State Stability (ISS)–Lyapunov analysis, ensuring uniform ultimate boundedness (UUB) of tracking errors. Extensive high-fidelity six-degree-of freedom (6-DOF) Monte Carlo simulations demonstrate that the ACHOSMC–LSTM outperforms state-of-the-art neural baselines, achieving a 53.5% reduction in Integrated Time Absolute Error (ITAE) and an 18.2% decrease in terminal miss distance. Furthermore, embedded hardware implementation on an NVIDIA Jetson Nano confirms operational feasibility with an average inference latency of 3.82 ms, validating the system's suitability for real-time onboard deployment. © 1965-2011 IEEE.},
