	abstract = {Sudden Cardiac Arrest (SCA) remain one of the most critical life-threatening emergencies that demands immediate detection and a rapid medical response. Existing ECG monitoring systems are heavily dependent on smartphones, cloud servers, or uninterrupted internet connectivity, which severely limits their reliability in rural areas and emergency scenarios where network access cannot be guaranteed. This paper present Nightfall-EX Advanced, a fully offline and portable electrocardiogram (ECG) monitoring and emergency alert system that incorporates TinyML-based arrhythmia detection directly on an embedded microcontroller. The proposed system integrates a AD8232 ECG front-end for cardiac signal acquisition, a ESP32 microcontroller for on-device signal processing and inference, a SIM800L GSM module for SMS-based emergency communication, and a NEO-7M GPS module for real-time location tracking. ECG signals are acquired, filtered, and classified locally using a lightweight quantized TinyML model trained on the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database, eliminating any cloud dependency entirely. A manual emergency trigger additionally enables the user to initiates reliable transmission of alert messages embedded with GPS coordinates. Experimental evaluation of the system demonstrated a overall classification accuracy of 93.4 %, with a precision of 9 2. 1 % and recall of 9 1. 3 % for abnormal rhythm detection. Average inference latency was measured between 18-22 ~ms, with RAM usage consistently remaining below 120 kB throughout all test conditions. Emergency SMS alerts were successfully delivered within 8-1 5 seconds across all trials conducted. These results confirm that the system is well suited for deployment in rural healthcare settings, elderly patient monitoring, and emergency medical response environments where offline and autonomous operation is essential © 2026 IEEE.},
