	doi = {10.1109/ICRAS70064.2026.11717989},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055296422?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The stability and safety of tunnel infrastructure are critical to modern transportation networks, and concrete crack detection is a core task of tunnel structural health inspection. To address the issues of high false positives and localization degradation of ultra-fine cracks in complex tunnel environments, this paper proposes a robust crack detection algorithm based on improved YOLOv8 for tunnel inspection robots. The algorithm embeds the Efficient Multi-Scale Attention (EMA) module into the backbone of YOLOv8n to suppress background noise such as water stains and precisely extract topological features of slender cracks. Meanwhile, an NWD-CIoU hybrid loss function is designed to mitigate the gradient vanishing problem in ultra-fine crack detection, ensuring extremely precise boundary localization. Experimental results demonstrate that compared with the baseline YOLOv8n model, the proposed algorithm steadily improves precision by 0.9 percentage points, mean average precision (mAP) at an IoU threshold of 0.5 by 1.3 percentage points, and the stringent mAP across IoU thresholds from 0.5 to 0.95 by 3.7 percentage points. Although the recall rate has a deliberate 3.8-percentage-point reduction as a design trade-off, via a conservative noise filtering strategy, it successfully minimizes false alarms with a negligible parameter increase of 0.02 M. Furthermore, edge deployment tests on an NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano achieve a real-Time inference speed of 42 frames per second (FPS). The algorithm effectively balances high-precision automated screening with computational efficiency, providing a robust, deployable visual perception solution that mitigates operator alert fatigue for tunnel inspection robots.  © 2026 IEEE.},
