	doi = {10.1109/ICESC70182.2026.11687500},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052177193?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence of Things (AIoT) has been rapidly expanding and bringing transformative features to the monitoring of vulnerable populations, notably in the area of women safety and elderly care. Yet, the computational and memory burden of state-of-the-art deep learning architectures is a major challenge for operating these predictive models on the edge devices with limited resources. Moreover, conventional deep neural networks are "black-box"models, and in safety-critical applications, such as medical settings, where the mechanism behind an emergency alert is as crucial as the alert itself, trust and clinical compliance are important issues that should not be overlooked. In this paper, a novel hybrid architecture is proposed to combine an Attention-Augmented Gated Recurrent Unit (A-GRU) with a Neuro-Symbolic Explainability Framework (NSEF). The system proposed not only has the strict computation efficiency for edge deployment in low power environments, but it can also produce human readable, rule-based explanations for automated safety interventions. The proposed A-GRU-NSEF framework has been evaluated on a synthetic hybrid dataset combining kinematic patterns obtained from the MobiAct v2 and physiological stress patterns obtained from WESAD, and it yields an excellent accuracy of 98.54%, while having a very low edge inference latency of 42.3 ms on an NVIDIA Jetson Nano platform when compiled using TensorRT. The results demonstrate a scalable, computationally efficient, and completely transparent paradigm for proactive edge-based threat assessment. © 2026 IEEE.},
