	doi = {10.1109/TCE.2026.3746590},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055505429?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Urban traffic congestion carries a growing environmental cost, and signal controllers at intersections need to be efficient, sustainable, and light enough to run on edge hardware rather than the cloud. Most reinforcement learning (RL) approaches to traffic signal control (TSC) optimize delay alone and say little about memory, latency, or power budgets, which makes them hard to justify for embedded deployment. This paper presents an energy-aware, multi-objective RL agent built for that setting. The agent combines Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) with a recurrent LSTM layer to track how traffic evolves over time, trained and evaluated in the SUMO microscopic simulator. Its architecture is sized against theoretical edge-hardware envelopes (M ≤ 50 MB, T<sub>inf</sub> ≤ 100 ms, P ≤ 10 W), and its reward function scalarizes four goals at once: minimizing vehicle delay, reducing CO<sub>2</sub> emissions, discouraging high-frequency phase switching, and penalizing excessively long green phases. Under heavy congestion, the trained controller cuts average delay by 25.28%, CO<sub>2</sub> emissions by 13.62%, and phase switches by 33.27% against a fixed-time baseline, with a coefficient of variation below 4% (3.56% in late-stage convergence) across four independently trained seeds. Post-quantization, the policy’s memory footprint measures 0.43 MB, comfortably inside the target envelope, while inference latency and power were profiled on an x86 platform as a proxy for the target ARM-class hardware, consistent with a hardware-informed and edge-compatible framework in model parameter footprint and computational complexity. Taken together, these results point to lightweight, hardware-informed RL as a practical direction for green, low-latency traffic control on edge-based Intelligent Transportation Systems, with on-device validation on embedded accelerators as the natural next step. © 1975-2011 IEEE.},
	type = {Article},
