	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047812831?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The rotor motor bearings of electric multi-rotor UAVs are subject to combined excitation from aerodynamic disturbances and airframe-coupled vibrations. When diagnostic models trained under laboratory conditions are deployed in real flight scenarios, distribution shift destabilizes classification boundaries. This cross-domain generalization problem is the core obstacle limiting intelligent bearing diagnosis in practice. To address this, we propose a cross-domain bearing fault diagnosis framework based on a soft prompt-guided graph neural network and domain-adaptive feature alignment. The framework maps time-domain, frequency-domain, and time–frequency features of vibration signals to graph nodes. A domain-aware graph structure modeling method based on soft prompt embedding is developed: domain-aware soft prompt vectors are embedded during node initialization, enabling the adaptive adjacency matrix to be dynamically constructed under dual constraints from signal feature correlations and domain membership. A dynamic prompt generation network, driven by batch statistics, together with an exponential moving average mechanism for stabilizing target-domain statistics under non-stationary conditions and small batch sizes, eliminates dependence on explicit domain labels. A cross-domain diagnostic optimization method based on attention-weighted maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) is also proposed: an attention weight network generates dimension-wise weight vectors to construct a weighted MMD loss, coordinated with a progressive scheduling strategy to balance classification and alignment objectives. Experiments across cross-speed, cross-load, and cross-dataset transfer tasks on five public benchmark datasets and one local multi-rotor UAV dataset, supplemented by a multi-physics simulation study, demonstrate that the proposed framework attains competitive Macro- (Formula presented) (Formula presented) 1 scores among the comparison methods, consistently achieving the highest Macro- (Formula presented) (Formula presented) 1 across the five public benchmark datasets. Ablation studies validate the necessity of each core component, and noise robustness analysis confirms that the framework maintains leading performance with small variance under low signal-to-noise ratios, demonstrating its effectiveness and robustness. Deployment efficiency analysis on Intel Core i7 and NVIDIA Jetson Xavier NX platforms confirms millisecond-level inference latency that meets typical UAV onboard requirements. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the IOP-Standard License.},
