	abstract = {Mobile robots with embedded devices provide a solution for the automated inspection of tunnel lining defects. However, deploying heavy models toward low-latency inference on constrained hardware remains challenging. To address this issue, this paper proposes an embedded deployment pipeline based on a lightweight deep learning (DL) model for real-time detection of lining cracks, spalling, and water leakage. A high-performance network is first constructed using a feature-adaptive weighted-fusion (FAWF) mechanism and an information-sharing backbone (ISB). Subsequently, a stepwise compression strategy is then applied to eliminate redundant parameters by integrating channel-wise distillation (CWD), channel-level sparsity (CLS), and layer-adaptive magnitude-based pruning (LAMP), yielding a compact and efficient model. The optimized model is accelerated with TensorRT and deployed on the mobile robot for performance evaluation of its real-time inference in a scaled tunnel lining with typical defects. Experimental results show that the compressed model achieves mAP@0.5 and F1 score of 0.752 and 0.731, reduces parameters, model size, and FLOPs by 80.17%, 64.92% and 76.64% compared with the original model, and maintains an inference speed exceeding 30 frames per second (FPS) in mobile-robot tests. This study introduces a novel approach for highway tunnel lining surface defect detection, demonstrating high performance on embedded devices and its potential for intelligent infrastructure inspection. © 2026},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Final},
