	abstract = {To address the challenges of high temporal modeling latency, redundant feature extraction, and limited cross-subject generalization in surface electromyography (sEMG) decoding for lower-limb exoskeleton control, this paper proposes a lightweight intention recognition network, NAGE-Net. The framework employs a parameterized adaptive Gabor encoder to extract physiologically informative frequency-band features and incorporates a spatiotemporal saliency enhancement module to strengthen responses of key spatial channels and temporal segments. By replacing traditional recurrent structures with Dilated Causal Gated Blocks (DCG-Block), the model overcomes sequential computation bottlenecks, while a two-stage fine-tuning strategy significantly improves cross-subject adaptability. Experimental results show that NAGE-Net outperforms existing state-of-the-art models on the UCI lower-limb sEMG dataset and achieves an accuracy of 97.48%, R<sup>2</sup> of 0.922, and RMSE of 6.78° on the in-house Noraxon dataset. Compared with mainstream models, NAGE-Net reduces parameter count, floating-point operations (FLOPs), and inference latency by 16.4%, 13.0%, and 37.7%, respectively. Moreover, the model provides clear time-frequency interpretability, offering a robust and transparent solution for real-time edge deployment in exoskeleton control systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
