	abstract = {The autonomous navigation of Unmanned Ground Vehicles (UGVs) with omnidirectional traction demands precise dynamic models capable of capturing complex phenomena involving friction and lateral slip. This article presents a methodological benchmark evaluating four data-driven system identification strategies: a nonlinear parametric model (Hammerstein), Linear Parameter-Varying (LPV-ARX) systems, the Koopman Operator via Extended Dynamic Mode Decomposition (EDMD), and Sparse Identification of Nonlinear Dynamics (SINDy). Experimentation was conducted on the Hiwonder ROSOrin hardware, equipped with Mecanum wheels and an onboard Jetson Orin NX edge computing module. To rigorously assess generalization capabilities and penalize parametric overfitting, the primary evaluation was structured under a bidirectional physical domain shift, alternating training and open-loop validation between a high-friction surface (grass) and a low-friction surface (smooth floor). The results demonstrated that EDMD and LPV-ARX achieved comparable global predictive precision across scenarios (RMSE ≈ 0.016–0.018) with inference latencies of ≈0.001 ms per step, maintaining consistent cross-domain predictive performance without retraining on the validation surface. In contrast, sparse regression techniques (SINDy) showed weaker cross-domain generalization. The EDMD-Koopman formulation provided comparable predictive performance to LPV-ARX while using a more parsimonious representation (54 active parameters compared to 84 for the LPV). To assess independent-run consistency, an extended validation using 18 independently acquired runs under mixed surface conditions was conducted, providing additional evidence of consistent predictive performance for the dimensional expansion approaches relative to sparse regression. The study concludes by discussing the suitability of EDMD and LPV formulations for future embedded predictive control architectures. © 2026 by the authors.},
