	abstract = {The fast development of Industry 5.0 requires smart robots that can have adaptive reasoning, autonomous programming, and real-time decision-making in the dynamic manufacturing setting. Traditional rule-based and fixed reinforcement learning (RL) methods do not have semantic comprehension and contextual adaptability, so they cannot be implemented in complex industrial environments. This paper is a proposal of a Reinforcement-Enhanced Large Language Model-based Intelligent Robotics Programming Framework (RELLM-IRP) to autonomous industrial automation to address these challenges. The framework combines semantic task decomposition based on LLM and hierarchical reinforcement learning, safety-constrained optimization and edge-adaptive deployment. A closed-loop semantic-to-control reinforcement pipeline converts high-level natural language instructions into structured control policies. Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) and Constrained Policy Optimization (CPO) guarantee compliance to collision avoidance, torque limits, and safety constraints of the operation. Simulation of a digital twin, model-based RL, and federated edge learning on a hybrid architecture allow self-correcting and context-aware robotic behavior. Experimental performance of collaborative robotic arms and mobile industrial robots show a 25-30x accelerated convergence rate and more than 95% task completion accuracy over PPO and SAC baseline. Safety measures indicate that about 40% of unsafe acts are minimized with all the critical failures eradicated. With less than 2 percent loss in accuracy, edge deployment with pruning and quantization results in a 45 % model size reduction, a 47% inference latency decrease, and a 36% reduction in power usage. The statistical confirmation is that it has a better learning stability and operational robustness in dynamic scenarios. The given framework develops a scalable, secure, and energy-efficient AI-controlled industrial robotics paradigm. Altogether, explainable, adaptive and resource-conscious automation of next-generation smart manufacturing ecosystems is promoted by RELLM-IRP. © 2026 IEEE.},
