	abstract = {With the increasing penetration of distributed energy resources, distribution-end operation is becoming more dynamic, decentralized, and time-sensitive, creating a growing need for local control and optimization at grid-edge nodes. However, although smart meters have been widely deployed, they still mainly function as data-acquisition terminals rather than executable carriers of local intelligence. To address this issue, this paper proposes an Edge Intelligence Gateway (EIG) for smart metering nodes in Electricity-IoT applications. The proposed EIG upgrades the metering endpoint from a passive sensing terminal to a deployable edge-control carrier by integrating multi-protocol communication, event-driven local control, native on-device optimization, and a low-code engineering workflow within one embedded platform. In the proposed framework, heterogeneous field data are unified through point-and-channel abstraction, local logic is organized through an Activity-on-Edge (AOE) mechanism, and optimization tasks are triggered and executed directly on the endpoint as part of the local control loop. The proposed architecture is validated through a field deployment in Zhejiang, China. Comparative results show that, relative to a conventional centralized architecture, the EIG achieves substantially lower end-to-end latency and better scalability, while enabling endpoint-native execution of optimization-driven applications such as local economic dispatch and demand response. These results demonstrate an engineering-feasible route for scalable edge intelligence at smart metering nodes.  © 2026 The Authors.},
