	abstract = {The proliferation of the Internet of Things (IoT) has made everyday devices such as cameras, TVs, and vehicles vulnerable to cyberattacks that can compromise privacy, safety, or physical assets. Securing these devices requires autonomous, on-device network monitoring, which in turn demands efficient and robust network intrusion detection systems (NIDSs). Machine learning (ML) enables NIDS to learn from diverse IoT traffic and attacks, but supporting multiple data sources typically requires larger models. In this work, we propose IOTree, an integer-only NIDS designed for resource-constrained IoT devices. IOTree substantially reduces memory footprint and inference latency with minimal impact on detection performance compared to traditional floating-point models. Experiments on IoT hardware show up to 81% faster inference and 48% lower memory usage, and IOTree outperforms recent related works in terms of efficiency, demonstrating the feasibility of more robust yet efficient on-device NIDS for resource-constrained IoT devices.  © 2026 IEEE.},
