	doi = {10.31763/ijrcs.v6i3.2753},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046625571?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Integrating legacy industrial robot arms into modern manufacturing environments is challenging due to limited interoperability, lack of Internet of Things (IoT) connectivity, and absence of AI-based perception capabilities. This study presents a low-cost non-invasive retrofit framework for automated sorting using a legacy OTC Daihen Almega AX-V6 industrial robot arm integrated with YOLOv8n-based visual perception, Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) communication, and edge-based supervisory monitoring. The research contribution is the development of a unified retrofit architecture combining AI-based segmentation, centroid-based coordinate mapping, MQTT-enabled coordination, and legacy industrial robot manipulation without modification of the proprietary robot controller. The proposed system integrates a conveyor belt, camera, NVIDIA Jetson Nano edge-computing platform, and web-based dashboard within a perception-driven robotic sorting workflow. Experimental evaluation was conducted under structured conveyor-based conditions involving varying object placement, moderate illumination variation, partial occlusion, and object-appearance variation. The proposed perception framework achieved a segmentation mAP50 of 99.5% with an average integrated inference latency of 807.8 ms on the Jetson Nano plat-form. Across 136 evaluated automatic sorting cycles involving 207 objects, the system achieved an overall physical sorting success rate of 99.5%. The single observed failure was associated with manipulation-induced displacement of a neighboring object during close-proximity pickup operation rather than AI perception or MQTT communication failure, highlighting the influence of legacy manipulation dynamics on retrofit reliability. The system main-tained stable operation during approximately 18 hours of extended runtime demonstrations without observable crashes or thermal throttling. These results demonstrate the practical feasibility of integrating AI-based perception, lightweight MQTT coordination, and legacy industrial robotic manipulation within a unified low-cost retrofit framework for structured low-throughput industrial conveyor environments. © 2026 The Authors. Published by Association for Scientific Computing Electrical and Engineering.},
