	abstract = {Recent advancements in embedded machine learning and the Internet of Things (IoT) has enabled the development of real-time assistive communication technologies. This paper presents a low-cost, portable, real-time sign language mediator that operates on an ESP32 microcontroller using a single flex sensor and a gyroscopic. Unlike multi-sensor gloves and vision-based methods, the proposed system achieves real-time recognition with an average end-to-end latency of 150–200 ms, while maintaining 99.32% classification accuracy across five predefined gestures. The design minimizes hardware complexity, reduces cost, and supports offline inference without cloud dependence, making it well suited for deployment in low-resource environments. By integrating gesture-to-text and speech feedback through a locally hosted web interface, the system extends accessibility and usability across devices. This work contributes to SDG 10 (Reduced Inequalities) by demonstrating how minimalist hardware and efficient embedded ML can advance inclusive communication technologies for the hearing-impaired. Unlike earlier ESP32 systems that rely on external PCs, the proposed model performs complete on-device inference and delivers synchronized text-and-speech feedback for inclusive communication. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
