	abstract = {Indoor scene recognition is a fundamental capability for service robots operating in domestic environments, yet remains challenging under variable illumination and the resource constraints of edge hardware. This paper presents EOSR (Edge-Optimized Scene Recognition), a framework integrating a four-stage dual-domain preprocessing pipeline with a channel-attention-augmented ResNet18 network for deployment on Raspberry Pi 5. The preprocessing pipeline sequentially applies Haar wavelet denoising, brightness normalization, white-balance correction, and CLAHE enhancement to suppress noise and normalize illumination prior to inference. A Squeeze-and-Excitation module inserted after the final residual stage recalibrates channel-wise feature responses to amplify scene-discriminative activations. Trained on a seven-category subset of MIT Indoor-67 and deployed via ONNX Runtime, EOSR achieves 83.45% Top-1 accuracy with 89.52 ms end-to-end latency and 11.17 FPS throughput, outperforming the ResNet18 baseline by 1.44% in accuracy while reducing latency by 41.4%. Robustness evaluations under seven synthetic lighting conditions and five Gaussian noise levels confirm consistent accuracy advantages. The preprocessing pipeline incurs only 24.13 ms overhead (27.0% of total latency), demonstrating that illumination robustness can be achieved at acceptable computational cost for real-time edge deployment. © 2026 IEEE.},
