	abstract = {This article presents a distributed thermal sensing and cloud–edge collaborative intelligent framework for real-time classification of indoor moisture and humidity. In this work, multiple FLIR Lepton 3.5 radiometric thermal sensors are deployed across humidity-prone locations, such as kitchens, showers, basements, and window areas in the home, to monitor moisture-induced temperature patterns continuously. Each thermal sensor node is connected to a Raspberry Pi 4B for data acquisition and wireless transmission to a GPU-enabled cloud server. Then, a lightweight, resource-efficient deep learning (DL) classifier is trained on the distributed thermal sensing data. In this work, we curated a novel dataset, thermal humidity extraction and recognition for maintenance in the indoor domain (THERMID), collected from multiple locations and comprising 22 classes of moisture, cold-spot, condensation, and temperature-state conditions. Furthermore, the proposed lightweight, resource-efficient DL model is centrally trained using the aggregated thermal sensing data and deployed on Rock Pi C+ edge devices for low-latency, on-device inference. The proposed lightweight DL model outperforms existing state-of-the-art pretrained models, providing 98.91% test accuracy with only a 2.6-MB model size and requiring only 0.542 GFLOPs for inference. In addition, the model is evaluated on Raspberry Pi 4B and Rock Pi C+ edge modules and is observed to provide 273- and 314-ms inference per image, respectively. The proposed system provides a scalable and energy-efficient solution for autonomous indoor moisture diagnostics, supporting proactive building health monitoring and integration with smart home control systems. © 2001-2012 IEEE.},
