	abstract = {Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models are a promising foundation for Embodied AI, but their high inference cost hinders robotic deployment. On-device inference is limited by compute and energy budgets, while full edge offloading is sensitive to network fluctuations and edge contention. Device-edge co-inference is a flexible alternative, but existing designs lack unified support for heterogeneous VLA paradigms and energy-aware scheduling. We propose EcoVLA, an adaptive device-edge co-inference framework for VLA models that maximizes system energy efficiency under real-time constraints. It abstracts VLA models into a unified stage-level co-inference space, estimates latency and energy from offline profiling and online sensing, and dynamically selects the most energy-efficient real-time scheme, with a lightweight transmission mechanism reducing communication overhead. Experiments show that EcoVLA improves energy efficiency by up to 236% over existing approaches under a 20 Hz constraint while consistently satisfying the real-time service-level objective (SLO). © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2027.},
