	abstract = {Vehicle-to-Everything (V2X) safety services, such as collision warning, demand ultra-low latency and real-time processing. However, existing studies often rely on simplified simulations or lack full standard-compliant end-to-end (E2E) system integration, making it difficult to reproduce results on a consistent benchmark. This paper proposes a fully open-source and standards-aligned V2X testbed that complies with 3GPP 5G Standalone (SA) architecture and ETSI Multi-access Edge Computing (MEC) specifications. By integrating free5GC, UERANSIM, and OpenAirInterface MEC Platform (OAI-MEP), we establish a complete E2E API service chain from the UE through the 5G data plane to the MEC platform and applications. A collision detection service based on Time-to-Collision (TTC) is implemented, and performance is evaluated under two deployment scenarios: MEC in-system and Remote Cloud server. Experimental results show that MEC deployment achieves 4-5 ms RTT (P99 ≈ 5.1 ms), whereas Cloud deployment incurs approximately 10× higher latency (≈ 55-60 ms), demonstrating the necessity of edge deployment for safety-critical V2X services. © 2026 IEEE.},
