	abstract = {The proliferation of Large Language Models (LLMs) has enabled the rise of intelligent agents capable of autonomous reasoning, multimodal interaction, and collaborative task execution. This paper proposes a scalable architecture for LLM-based AI agents operating across distributed and collaborative ecosystems, integrating cloud orchestration, edge deployment, and device-level interaction. Our framework leverages intent-based communication pipelines, adaptive behavioural modelling, and multi-agent management to optimise performance across heterogeneous environments. Numerical simulations demonstrate that the proposed architecture reduces average latency from 120 ms (baseline REST-based agents) to 58ms using event-driven orchestration and gRPC protocols. Resource efficiency improves 70% to 45% and memory consumption from 8 GB to 4.8 GB. Agent discovery accuracy reaches 92.4% across 100 simulated agents, outperforming traditional polling-based systems by 18% points. A key application is in 6G-enabled smart manufacturing, where distributed agents coordinate real-time decisions across robotic arms, sensors, and cloud analytics. The architecture ensures low-latency, high-reliability communication while preserving privacy and scalability. © 2026, Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH. All rights reserved.},
