	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048741802?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {There is a rapidly growing demand for human-machine interfaces (HMIs) and autonomous navigation capabilities in everyday applications, especially in the field of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs). Nevertheless, such advanced features of computer vision and image processing algorithms are not yet widely available on standalone devices. In this paper, we propose that there is a lightweight and intelligent drone system that achieves object recognition and tracking as well as hand gesture recognition in real time based on edge computing technology. The integration hardware architecture includes a Pixhawk flight controller and Jetson Nano embedded computer, all of which are mounted on a large carbon fiber frame that guarantees long-lasting as well as stable deployment. Real-time image data provided by a camera module gets processed in an edge device with optimized deep learning models (SSD for object detection and MediaPipe for hand gesture recognition) to obtain flight control commands like takeoff, landing, hover, and tracking commands directly without the need of any controller or ground station. Based on flight tests and including initial evaluations, the system performs satisfactorily, considering low-latency control loops for different operating conditions, high recognition accuracy over a range of lighting environments, and reliable tracking during flight. This experimental study demonstrates the deploy ability of edge AI to enable continuous and completely self-sustaining human-machine interaction.  © 2026 IEEE.},
