	doi = {10.1109/RIVF68649.2025.11365235},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034482788?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {As drones become increasingly integrated into search and rescue operations, understanding the barriers that impact effectiveness and optimizing humandrone interaction is critical. This research presents the design and implementation of a remote sensing-based human detection system utilizing a custom-built coaxial unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) to support search and rescue (SAR) operations in post-disaster environments. Integrating autonomous flight control, RF signal-based localization, and deep learning-based visual recognition, the system enhances victim detection in complex and constrained terrains. This design offers superior aerodynamic efficiency, enhanced mechanical resilience, and stable flight performance in various environments. A lightweight MobileNet-SSD model is deployed and optimized for edge inference using a Jetson Nano device, enabling low-latency, real-time human detection (40 FPS) from aerial imagery. Specifically, the system is designed to operate from a UAV at an altitude of approximately 1 0 meters, where human subjects typically occupy less than 3 % of the image area, presenting a significant challenge for standard object detectors due to the tiny object size. Experimental validation demonstrates that combining coaxial aerial mobility with intelligent onboard sensing significantly improves situational awareness and victim localization efficiency. The proposed system represents a scalable, reliable, and high-performance aerial solution tailored for modern disaster response operations under extreme environmental and infrastructural constraints. © 2025 IEEE.},
