	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038018799?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper presents an autonomous, low-power, and scalable vehicle counting and monitoring system, both in terms of network and functionality, that does not depend on a continuous internet connection for its operation. The proposal integrates LoRa communication for long-distance transmission of counting data with high energy efficiency, edge processing using an NVIDIA Jetson Nano running a YOLOv8n-based detection model, and remote feature update capability through the decentralized InterPlanetary File System (IPFS) using hash addressing. The system implements bidirectional data flow over LoRa technology, allowing both the sending of information (counts, measurements) and the receiving of control commands (remote reboots, hash updates). Field evaluations demonstrate the system’s ability to detect and classify vehicles in real-time with low latency, maintain low power consumption, and update operational scripts with 100% success in test scenarios. On average, 8.92 FPS were achieved during inference and a full update deployment time of less than 1.2 minutes, validating the effectiveness, resilience, and functional scalability of this architecture for vehicle monitoring in diverse environments. © 2025 IEEE.},
