	abstract = {Reliable communication in post-disaster environments is often hindered by damaged infrastructure and the absence of centralized control, requiring autonomous and efficient operation at the network edge. Transport-layer flow management, particularly for TCP, becomes critical under such constraints. However, existing flow classification methods rely on server-side visibility and post-handshake data, rendering them ineffective in disconnected or delay-prone environments. This paper presents a client-side TCP flow classification approach that leverages only three-way handshake features. Our approach classifies flows early without traffic overhead. We evaluate several supervised learning models in terms of classification accuracy and resource efficiency. The LightGBM classifier achieves an optimal balance with 97.33% accuracy, low inference latency, and a minimal memory footprint, demonstrating the viability of edge deployment. Our results confirm that early client-side flow classification is both feasible and effective for timely flow identification in resource-constrained environments. © 2025 IEEE.},
