	abstract = {Recently, deep learning-based vehicle detection methods for urban roads have demonstrated significant advantages in autonomous driving. However, in scenarios where target scales are small and background information is complex, current methods suffer from low detection accuracy and cannot ensure real-time performance. To address this, this paper proposes a YOLO-based vehicle detection method for urban roads. First, to solve the feature information loss caused by the convolution stride and pooling in shallow networks, a more refined pooling structure is designed, stacking spatial information into the channel dimension, and efficient shallow feature sampling is achieved on the basis of step-free convolution. Moreover, an efficient feature extraction network is designed on the basis of the high-order spatial interaction concept of deep convolution to collect deep feature information from the network. Moreover, the reconstructed loss function improves the accuracy of target localization. Finally, a channel pruning strategy is employed to compress the model size. For the UA-DETRAC dataset, the proposed method improves the recall by 3.1% and the mAP by 3.5%, reduces the number of parameters to 32% of the original value, and achieves a real-time inference speed of 95.3 FPS. In addition, real-time performance verification was conducted on the NVIDIA Jetson TX2 embedded device, which achieved a detection speed of 18.5 FPS. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed model outperforms state-of-the-art lightweight models and meets the requirements for engineering applications. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag London Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2025.},
