	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105022736497?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {To improve the responsiveness and energy efficiency of electric vehicle (EV) thermal management systems under complex driving conditions, this paper presents an optimized framework that integrates predictive modeling and adaptive control. A multi-domain thermal architecture is proposed to couple heat sources and loads—such as battery, motor, power electronics, and cabin—enabling cross-domain energy coordination. A lightweight LSTM network enhanced by multihead spatio-temporal attention (MHSTA) is designed to predict driving scenarios using multi-source time-series inputs including vehicle dynamics, ambient conditions, and battery states. Channel pruning is applied to reduce model complexity, achieving 94.8% classification accuracy with an inference latency of only 12 ms, making it suitable for edge deployment. The control strategy employs a finite state machine (FSM) driven by predicted driving modes and expert rules to dynamically switch between thermal modes—such as waste heat recovery, heat pump coordination, and PTC-assisted heating—while PID-based smoothing ensures stable transitions. A co-simulation platform combining CoolSim is developed to evaluate the system under quasi-transient conditions. Results demonstrate that the proposed method reduces heating-related energy consumption by approximately 3% in cold-start mixed driving cycles, while maintaining cabin comfort and thermal stability. © 2025 SPIE.},
