	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032738855?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The Human BlackBox project introduces an AI-powered, smartphone-based system designed to autonomously detect emergencies and provide real-time alerts without relying on external hardware. Utilizing built-in sensors such as the microphone, camera, accelerometer, and gyroscope, the system continuously monitors the user's environment to identify critical situations including falls, crashes, and distress events. It employs lightweight, on-device deep learning models YAMNet for audio classification, LSTM for motion sequence analysis, and YOLOv8 for visual posture detection optimized for edge performance using TensorFlow Lite. Upon identifying an emergency, the Human BlackBox captures the preceding 60 seconds of multi-sensor data, securely stores it, and transmits automated notifications to pre-registered emergency contacts through Firebase Cloud Messaging. Designed with a strong focus on privacy, low latency, and scalability, the system ensures continuous protection even in low-connectivity environments. This innovation bridges the gap between personal safety and ubiquitous technology, making advanced emergency detection accessible to all smartphone users. By combining real-time sensor fusion, autonomous event recognition, and intelligent alert mechanisms, the Human BlackBox redefines personal safety solutions, offering an efficient, cost-effective, and universally deployable platform that transforms everyday devices into proactive life-saving tools. © 2025 IEEE.},
