	abstract = {We compared small language models (SLMs) and large language models (LLMs), focusing on their architectural foundations, performance characteristics, and deployment considerations. By examining distinctions in model size, structure, capacity, and optimization techniques, the results highlight the trade-offs between computational efficiency and generalization ability. While LLMs demonstrate superior accuracy, reasoning capabilities, and emergent behaviors due to their massive scale, SLMs offer advantages in speed, cost-effectiveness, and on-device deployment through innovations like distillation, quantization, and lightweight attention mechanisms. We also explored their strengths in accuracy, inference latency, safety, and real-world applications. The results indicated that the choice between SLMs and LLMs depends on the specific requirements of the task and operational environment. Finally, we discussed hybrid strategies that combine both models to achieve optimal performance across diverse use cases. © 2025 IEEE.},
