	abstract = {Road surface deterioration, particularly potholes, poses serious risks to traffic safety and vehicle integrity. Timely detection and localization are critical for effective maintenance and accident prevention. This paper presents a real-time road damage detection system focused on water-filled potholes, leveraging a deep learning approach based on the YOLOv7 object detection model. This work differentiates itself by addressing the specific challenge of detecting water-filled potholes-a task complicated by obscured visual cues-using a custom-annotated dataset tailored for this problem. The model was trained and optimized using the Tree-Structured Parzen Estimator (TPE) and deployed on Jetson AGX Orin platforms to validate its real-world feasibility. The optimized model achieved a mean Average Precision (mAP) of 0.7584 and an inference time of 16.7 ms, demonstrating the viability of an accurate, low-latency system in an edge computing environment. These findings demonstrate the feasibility of deploying an accurate, low-latency pothole detection system in edge computing environments. The proposed system has potential applications in smart transportation infrastructure, enabling proactive maintenance and enhanced road safety. © 2025 IEEE.},
