	doi = {10.1109/SustainCom67920.2025.00011},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033524933?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Reliable road hazard perception for autonomous driving demands millisecond-level responses to ensure the safety of drivers, passengers, pedestrians, and other road users. We present an on-device detection framework that conducts both feature extraction and decision making locally to reduce transmission overhead and achieve a favorable accuracy-latency trade-off. This paradigm reduces transmission overhead while maintaining high detection accuracy. At the heart of the framework is a compact, multi-scale, densely fused detector derived from the YOLO family, designed to exploit semantic information at multiple abstraction levels through cross-layer and cross-scale feature reuse. Such a design enhances sensitivity to small and partially occluded objects and improves robustness in diverse and complex traffic scenes, including varying weather and lighting conditions. Extensive experiments conducted on the BDD100K dataset and deployed on real edge hardware demonstrate that our approach achieves consistently higher average precision while substantially reducing both model size and inference latency compared to strong baselines, validating its suitability for real-Time autonomous driving perception.  © 2025 IEEE.},
