	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033679663?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Paint factories face difficulties in keeping up with occupational safety standards because of the hazardous pollutants they produce, such as toxic fumes, particulates, and volatile organic compounds (VOCs). In dynamic environments, traditional monitoring systems based on thresholds do not work well because of their high latency and rigidity. To address such limitations, we propose an improved safety monitoring system that is more sophisticated and utilizes AI and the Internet of Things. The system takes measurements of temperature, humidity, carbon monoxide, particulate matter, volatile organic compound (VOC), and particulate matter using industrial-grade IoT sensors to enable interpretation of the data. We use a convolutional neural network autoencoder (CNN-AE) for unsupervised anomaly detection, and a gradient boosting decision tree (GBDT) classifier for compliance classification into secure, warning, and critical levels. The functionality of the system was confirmed using more than 10.8 million sensor measurements in paint plant solvent storage, pigment mixing, and spray-coating regions. The hybrid CNN-AE + GBDT model performed better than all alternatives with an inference latency of 462 ms, 96.7<sup>%</sup> recall, 96.3 % precision, and 96.8% overall accuracy. The outcomes illustrate the possibility of leveraging IoT and AI to enable real-time, reliable safety monitoring in dangerous industrial environments. © 2025 IEEE.},
