	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050241666?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Internet of Vehicles (IoV) systems generate massive volumes of video streams, posing significant challenges to real-time decision-making and data management in resource-constrained autonomous driving scenarios. Existing video summarization methods often struggle to capture long-term dependencies due to inherent information redundancy and complex dynamic variations across frames. To address these issues, we propose a novel framework termed Position-Aware Spatio-Temporal Attention for Video Summarization (PSTA-VS). PSTA-VS enhances feature representations by progressively integrating multi-dimensional attention mechanisms to capture long-term dependencies effectively. Specifically, the framework introduces two core components: 1) Global Dependency Encoding (GDE), which models cross-dimensional spatiotemporal correlations across the entire sequence to preserve comprehensive contextual information; and 2) Position-Aware Attention (PAA), which adaptively assigns weights to critical frames and spatial regions while suppressing redundant content. By seamlessly fusing these mechanisms, PSTA-VS generates coherent, information-rich summaries. Experiments on two benchmark datasets demonstrate that PSTA-VS outperforms state-of-the-art methods. Inference latency and edge-deployment evaluations on two additional datasets further confirm its responsiveness and lightweight design. By avoiding complex stacked architectures, PSTA-VS offers an efficient and scalable solution for visual understanding in resource-limited IoV environments. © 2014 IEEE.},
