	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035990352?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Date-palm pruning is a hazardous and laborintensive task performed at heights among sharp fronds. This paper presents an embedded vision system to automate datepalm branch pruning by detecting branches and localizing precise cutting points using deep learning on an edge device. We propose a two-stage detect-to-cut pipeline: a YOLOv8 object detector identifies palm branches, and a YOLOv8-Pose model predicts keypoints corresponding to optimal cut locations. Both models are optimized for deployment on an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX, enabling real-time inference on the edge. In experiments, the system achieves high accuracy (branch detection mAP@0.5 of 93.58%, and cut-point PCK@0.2 of 79.9%) while maintaining an inference speed of 5.56 FPS on the embedded device. The results demonstrate a viable solution for precision robotic pruning, combining state-of-the-art vision algorithms with efficient edge computing. The proposed system could improve safety and efficiency in agricultural robotics by enabling autonomous pruning of date palms in field conditions. © 2025 IEEE.},
