	abstract = {The security risks associated with botnet and Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attacks have increased due to the Internet of Things' (IoT) devices' exponential growth in industries like smart homes, healthcare, and industrial control systems [1]-[3]. IoT devices with limited resources are especially susceptible, so real-time, lightweight anomaly detection systems are required. A hybrid Convolutional Neural Network and Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (CNN+BiLSTM) framework for real-time botnet and DDoS anomaly detection is presented in this paper. It is especially designed for edge deployment on devices like Raspberry Pi [4], [5]. The suggested system uses MQTT to ingest live IoT network traffic, preprocesses features in real time, and applies the learned model to locally identify anomalies. A dashboard based on Streamlit also offers an easy-to-use visual representation of threats that have been identified. Robust data preprocessing, model training with class imbalance handling and hyperparameter optimization, and real time inference at the edge are all components of the multi-phase implementation. For dynamic IoT environments, the system seeks to provide low-latency, scalable, and autonomous protection [6] [8]. Standard IoT datasets like UNSW-NB15 and BoT-IoT will be used for the evaluation. © 2025 IEEE.},
