	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035720577?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The growing sophistication of autonomous Mars operations has necessitated intelligent onboard systems that can respond to the harsh and unpredictable Martian environmental conditions. Remote control and delayed processing are no longer efficient enough for real-time decision-making. To meet the challenge, we propose a lightweight AI-based solution to onboard Martian environmental prediction. The project focuses on designing and deploying machine learning models that can accurately forecast environmental parameters collected from onboard rover sensors. The models are trained on actual Mars mission datasets and tested with the Edge Impulse platform, enabling end-to-end model development, optimization, and deployment testing on embedded systems. The models are optimized for edge deployment with low power and computational demands, supporting a broad spectrum of hardware - from C++ and Arduino-based microcontrollers to more sophisticated embedded systems like NVIDIA Jetson, STM32, BrainChip Akida, and ARM Ethos-U NPUs. This flexibility guarantees real-time inference capability on various rover platforms. The long-term goal is to improve the autonomy of rovers through the ability to monitor and predict environmental conditions in real time, thus increasing navigation safety, power efficiency, and mission effectiveness overall.  © 2025 IEEE.},
