	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105030540597?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This research develops a machine vision algorithm integrating deep learning feature extraction (ResNet-based), edge computing optimization (TensorRT), and fast reconstruction (ICP) to enhance precision control in flexible assembly systems. Through case studies in automotive and electronics manufacturing, we demonstrate the system's exceptional adaptability (rated 5/5 for electronics and medical assemblies) and high accuracy (99.2% in quality inspection). Edge computing implementation achieves 40% latency reduction (90 ms vs 150 ms for image processing), enabling real-time control critical for high-speed assembly lines. While the system shows robust performance across single-step tasks, multi-step operations exhibit marginal precision drops (pm 2.5 % error), identifying an area for future improvement. Post-optimization results show 50% reduction in error rates (1.1% vs 2.5% in automotive assembly), confirming the algorithm's effectiveness in reconfigurable manufacturing environments. The proposed solution addresses key challenges in modern smart manufacturing by combining deep learning's pattern recognition capabilities with edge computing's low-latency processing and rapid reconstruction techniques. These innovations significantly improve upon traditional cloud-based machine vision systems, particularly in time-sensitive assembly applications. Future research directions include integration with 5G networks to support decentralized manufacturing architectures and expansion to more complex multi-robot assembly scenarios. © 2025 IEEE.},
